// Runtime/StringRuntime.maxon — **THE FLOOR OF A `String`: the parts that were never method dispatch.**
//
// ⭐⭐ **W49 RETIRED THE WHOLE METHOD SURFACE ONTO `stdlib/String.maxon` — TWENTY-TWO MEMBERS ACROSS NINE
// WAVES — AND `dispatchStringMethod` NOW SERVES NONE.** What could not retire is what is here, because it
// was never a member to hand back: the RECORD a String IS, the LITERAL that materializes it, `==`, drop,
// clone, and the two byte doors a fused wrapper's inline `managed` opens. Wave 9 took the last member
// (`setByte`) and then took everything OUT of this file that a String was merely the loudest user of.
//
// This file owns THREE things and their single source is here:
//   1. the RECORD LAYOUT — the offsets every reader loads by and the offsets `LowerMaxonToStd` writes when
//      it assembles a literal's record. The first SIX are `BufferOwnership`'s whole buffer record, ALIASED
//      here rather than restated, because `__mm_cow_detach` and `__managed_decref` are single emitted
//      functions over both records; `singleByteGraphemes@48` is genuinely this record's own.
//      `buildStringRecordBytes` asserts each field lands at its named offset,
//      and `ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts` asserts the whole shape against
//      **the corpus's own `type String`** — which is the description that outranks this one.
//   2. the LITERAL — an immortal `.rdata` blob plus an immortal 56-byte record pointing at it, deduped by
//      bytes, with `buffer@0` patched by each object-file writer once `.rdata` has an address. Nothing is
//      allocated, refcounted or freed, so a literal-only program stays leak-gate-free.
//   3. the runtime GRAPHS a String needs and nothing else does — `__str_eq`, `__str_decref`,
//      `__str_clone`, the two constructors, the byte view, and interpolation's number rendering — built as
//      `StdOp` graphs exactly as `MmRuntime` builds the allocator, so they get register allocation, frames
//      and the whole backend for free.
//
// ⚠⚠ **WHAT LEFT AT WAVE 9, AND WHY EACH WAS NOT A STRING'S:**
//   * `Runtime/RuntimeUsage.maxon` — `RuntimeUsage`, `scanRuntimeUsage`, `recordCallUsage` and the four
//     record/close pairs: **760 lines** deciding the install of the scheduler, IOCP, subprocesses, arrays,
//     sets, maps, lists, descriptor and existential drops, union destructors, struct cloners, the wall
//     clock, files, directories, argv, stdin and process introspection. A String was one tenant of that.
//   * `MmRuntime` — `__str_copy` and the two `u8` load emitters. A byte blit and a byte load are Std-tier
//     operations; both emitters were ALREADY exported for `GraphemeRuntime`, which is the two-files-own-
//     the-one-spelling shape their own header claimed to prevent.
//   * `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ByteStringElementSize` absorbed `StringElementSize`. Since the envelope collapse a
//     String IS its `__ManagedMemory with Byte`, so its `element_size@24` is an `Array with Byte`'s — one
//     number, and only the survivor carries a derivation anything checks.

// --- The fused 56-byte String record (bootstrap `MaxonToStandardConversion.Helpers.cs:503-536`) ---
//
// Every field is an 8-byte slot, so the seven offsets are 0/8/16/24/32/40/48 and the record is 56 bytes.
// `buffer@0` is the ABSOLUTE VA of the byte blob — emitted as a PLACEHOLDER 0 and patched by the PE
// writer once `.rdata`'s RVA is known (a fixed-base image, so the VA is compile-time computable). The
// blob is a SEPARATE `.rdata` entry, NUL-terminated, and `length@8` EXCLUDES the NUL.
// ⭐⭐ **THE FIRST SIX SLOTS ARE A WHOLE `__ManagedMemory`, EMBEDDED, AND SINCE W157 THAT IS LITERAL RATHER
// THAN NEARLY-TRUE.** They are `BufferOwnership`'s own constants — `__mm_cow_detach` and `__managed_decref`
// are single emitted functions that read them out of BOTH records — so the numbers exist once and these
// names alias them. `@40` was the one slot that disagreed (`singleByteGraphemes` here, `element_destroy`
// there); it now holds a real `element_destroy`, always `TrivialElementDestroy`, and the flag moved to a
// seventh slot at `@48`. That is what makes a String record servable to buffer code as-is.
export let StringBufferOffset = ManagedRecordBufferOffset
export let StringLengthOffset = ManagedRecordLengthOffset

// Exported for `Parser.emitStringInterp`, which WRITES an owned heap String's record (the literal path
// here only ever writes rdata). One set of offsets, read by the runtime and written by the interpolation
// lowering, so a reader and a writer of the same record cannot drift.
// ⭐ **THESE THREE ARE DERIVED FROM `BufferOwnership`, NOT DECLARED HERE**, because `__mm_cow_detach` is ONE
// emitted function that reads them out of this record AND out of the Array record. They were two
// coincidentally-equal sets of literals until that function existed; a divergence would now be a wrong load
// inside it rather than a second value flowing through a parameter, so the numbers live in one place and
// these names alias them.
export let StringCapacityOffset = ManagedRecordCapacityOffset
export let StringElementSizeOffset = ManagedRecordElementSizeOffset
let StringParentPtrOffset = ManagedRecordParentOffset

// ⭐ **`element_destroy@40` — A STRING'S IS ALWAYS `TrivialElementDestroy`, AND THAT IS A FACT RATHER THAN
// A FILLER.** A String's elements are `Byte`s; a byte owns nothing, so there is no per-element destructor
// to run and the drop path releases the bytes without walking them. Aliased from the shared offset for the
// reason the five above are: `__managed_decref` reads this slot out of a String record now (W157 —
// `__str_bytes_view` hands one over), so a divergence would be a wrong load in a function that cannot tell
// which record it was handed.
let StringElementDestroyOffset = ManagedRecordElementDestroyOffset

// ⭐⭐ **SLOT@48 IS `singleByteGraphemes` — *EVERY GRAPHEME IN THIS STRING IS EXACTLY ONE BYTE* — AND IT
// USED TO BE THE STRICTLY WEAKER `isAscii`.** The corpus field this record IS
// (`stdlib/String.maxon:86`'s `singleByteGraphemesFlag`) has always carried the stronger fact, and its own
// declaration records the cost of the confusion: *"this field used to say only 'is ASCII', and ten methods
// read THAT as 'one byte per character' … all ten split the cluster"*. **CR+LF is a SINGLE cluster under
// UAX #29 GB3**, so an all-ASCII string is not necessarily one byte per grapheme.
//
// ⚠ **WHILE THE TWO MEANINGS DISAGREED, `String.hasSingleByteGraphemes()` HAD TO ANSWER A CONSERVATIVE
// `false` OUTRIGHT** — serving `isAscii` there would have made the corpus's `countGraphemes` take the
// shortcut and return the BYTE length, 2, for a CR+LF string where every other path in all three compilers
// says 1. That refusal cost SPEED on long ASCII strings (measured by the corpus at ×2.4 rising to ×6.5 on
// `findFirst`/`findLast`/`slice-by-length`), and `Parser.dispatchStringMethod` named the cure exactly:
// *"strengthening the record's flag to the corpus's meaning at its literal scan and at all nine of its
// propagation sites"*. That is what this is.
//
// ⭐ **CR-FREE RATHER THAN CRLF-FREE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT COMPOSABLE** — *"no CR"* is a property of
// each byte ON ITS OWN, so it survives both slicing and concatenation and a concatenation may simply AND the
// two flags. *"No CRLF pair"* would not: `"a\r"` and `"\nb"` each lack one and their join has one.
// ⭐⭐ **THE FLAG SITS BEHIND THE WHOLE BUFFER RECORD, WHICH IS WHY THE OFFSET IS `ManagedRecordBytes`
// RATHER THAN `48`.** That is the one fact that decides it: `@48` is not a number this file picked, it is
// where a `__ManagedMemory` ENDS. Widen the buffer record and this follows; write `48` and it does not.
export let StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset = ManagedRecordBytes as ByteOffset

// A String record is a whole buffer record plus this one slot. ⚠ **IT ALIASED `ManagedRecordBytes`
// OUTRIGHT UNTIL W157**, when the two records were the same six slots disagreeing about what `@40` held;
// the alias is now an ADDITION, and that difference is the rung. It is still DERIVED rather than written
// `56`, for the same reason: the number is "a buffer record, plus a flag".
let StringFieldBytes = 8 as BytePos

export let StringRecordBytes = ManagedRecordBytes + StringFieldBytes

// ⚠ **`StringElementSize = 1` STOOD HERE AND IS GONE (W49 wave 9).** It said *"a String's elements are
// bytes"*, which since the envelope collapse is `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ByteStringElementSize` in other words — a
// String IS its `__ManagedMemory with Byte`, so the stride its four producers stamp at `element_size@24`
// IS an `Array with Byte`'s. Two constants held one number and only the survivor carried a derivation:
// `ByteStringElementSize` is CHECKED against `ProgramSignatures.arrayElementSize`'s answer for that
// instance, and is the number `byteStringBlobStrideMessage`'s gate compares against. This one was a bare
// literal under a comment. The four readers moved onto the checked one.
//
// ⭐ **A LITERAL'S `capacity@16`/`parent@32` ARE THE SHARED BUFFER-OWNERSHIP ENCODING'S** (`BufferOwnership`),
// not this file's invention and not the Array record's either: `ImmortalRecordCapacity` says the buffer is an
// immortal `.rdata` blob that is not this record's to write or to release, and `NoOwedAllocation` says the
// record owes no separate allocation a release. The Array record's byte-string literal writes exactly the
// same pair for exactly the same reason.

// ⭐ AN OWNED INTERPOLATION RESULT IS A HEAP STRING, and its bytes live INLINE — right after the 56-byte
// record, in the SAME `__mm_alloc` payload (`buffer@0 = record + StringRecordBytes`). So the whole thing
// is one allocation: `__mm_decref` → `__mm_free` reclaims the record AND its bytes together, and the
// destructor a struct passes (`TrivialDestructor`, 0) is exactly right — there is no external buffer to
// free. `capacity@16` is `InlineBufferCapacity` and `parent@32` is `NoOwedAllocation` (unwritten — the
// zeroing contract supplies it), which is the shared encoding saying exactly that: not mine to write in
// place, and no separate allocation owed. See `Parser.emitStringInterp`.
//
// The fixed bytes an owned interpolation result allocates BEYOND its text: the 56-byte record the bytes
// live inline behind, plus one trailing NUL (which the slab's zeroing supplies for free — nothing writes
// it). `Parser.emitStringInterp` sizes its one `__mm_alloc` as `totalLen + StringInterpOverheadBytes`.
export let StringInterpOverheadBytes = (StringRecordBytes as ParsedInt) + 1

// `singleByteGraphemes@48` — 1 iff every byte is < 128 AND no byte is a CR (a compile-time scan). An empty
// string satisfies it vacuously. The high-bit threshold and the two flag values are named rather than bare
// so the scan reads as the property it computes.
let AsciiHighBit = 128 as Byte
let SingleByteGraphemesTrue = 1 as ParsedInt
let SingleByteGraphemesFalse = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **U+000D, AND THIS IS THE THIRD STATEMENT OF THE BYTE — the corpus already carries two, WITH a measured
// reason, and this one has the same shape of reason.** `stdlib/helpers/string/grapheme.maxon:38` is the
// AUTHORITY (it implements GB3); `stdlib/String.maxon:53` states it a second time because its classifier
// tests every byte of every string as it is constructed and a cross-file call per byte measured worse than
// the second walk it replaced. This copy exists because the scan below runs in the COMPILER, at lower time,
// over a literal's bytes — it cannot call corpus Maxon at all. What holds the three together is a CHECK and
// not a comment: `specs-shv2/grapheme-clusters`'s `grapheme-boundary-crlf` and the literal-flag cases go red
// if any of them stops treating CR specially.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED FOR `GraphemeRuntime.buildCharFromCodepoint`, WHICH IS THE OTHER PRODUCER OF THIS FIELD.**
// Slot@48's meaning is declared in this file, so the byte that clears it is declared here too — a second
// `13` in the encoder would be a fourth statement of the number with nothing holding it to this one.
export let CarriageReturnByte = 13 as Byte

// The blob is the payload bytes followed by a single NUL — a C string, so the record's pointer is
// usable by any consumer that wants a NUL-terminated buffer, while `length@8` is what Maxon reads.
// ⚠ EXPORTED — and `export` rather than `module` is the correct one, which the self-compile's own
// visibility check confirms by NOT flagging it. The blob's READER needs the same terminator its writer
// used: `TargetPrinter` renders a blob's text into a golden and must stop where this stops, not at the
// padding — and it lives in `Compiler/IR/Target/`, outside this file's `Compiler/Runtime/` subtree, which
// is exactly the reach `module` does not have (measured: `module` here is `E2004 Undefined variable`).
export let NulTerminator = 0 as Byte

// `__int_to_string`'s decimal machinery. The digits are rendered without ever NEGATING the value, so
// `i64.min` is handled with no overflow: signed `%`/`/` keep the value's sign (`x % 10 ∈ [-9,0]` for a
// negative `x`), and the per-digit magnitude is recovered by MULTIPLYING by `sign = 1 - 2*neg` (`+1` for
// a non-negative value, `-1` for a negative one) rather than by an `abs` that `i64.min` would overflow.
let AsciiMinusSign = 45 as ParsedInt

// `value >>logical 63` is `1` exactly when the sign bit is set — the branch-free `isNegative` the sign
// and the leading `-` are both derived from.
let SignBitPosition = 63 as ParsedInt
let SignMultiplierBase = 1 as ParsedInt
let SignMultiplierSpan = 2 as ParsedInt

// The scratch an integer part reserves before `__int_to_string` fills it: `i64.min` renders as
// "-9223372036854775808", 20 digits and a sign = 21 bytes, and no i64 is wider. `Parser.emitStringInterp`
// allocates this, the converter fills `len ≤ 21` bytes, and the caller frees it after the copy.
//
// ⚠ **NOT `export`ED, AND W49 WAVE 9 IS WHY IT STOPPED BEING** — an orphan sweep found no cross-file
// reader for either scratch budget, exactly the failure `buildStrSplit`'s grave warns about (*"an `export`
// outlives its last cross-file caller silently — nothing in this language reports one"*). The one door is
// `integerToStringScratchBytes(signedness)` below, and closing this one MAKES the argument in its header
// true rather than merely urged: a second selection site can no longer name the wrong budget, because it
// can no longer name a budget at all.
let IntToStringScratchBytes = 21 as ParsedInt

// The scratch the UNSIGNED renderer reserves (X11): `u64.max` renders as "18446744073709551615", 20
// digits and NO sign, and no u64 is wider. It is a constant of its own rather than the signed budget
// reused, because the two are derived from two different worst cases and the byte the difference is
// made of — the sign — is precisely the byte this renderer never writes. Pairing it with the wrong
// name is what `integerToStringScratchBytes` exists to make impossible — see its twin above for why
// neither is `export`ed any more.
let UIntToStringScratchBytes = 20 as ParsedInt

// `__bool_to_string` renders "true" (4) or "false" (5). The ASCII bytes of both, and the two lengths.
//
// ⚠ `AsciiLowerA` was `export`ed for a SECOND reader — the parser's `toLower`/`toUpper` arms, which handed
// the gated range and the shift to `__str_map_case`. Those retired onto `stdlib/String.maxon` in W49 wave 2
// and the five constants that served them went with them, leaving this the byte `a` of "false" and nothing
// else. Non-exported again, so the file that owns the only reader owns the only name.
let AsciiLowerT = 116 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerR = 114 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerU = 117 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerE = 101 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerF = 102 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerA = 97 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerL = 108 as ParsedInt
let AsciiLowerS = 115 as ParsedInt
let BoolTrueLength = 4 as ParsedInt
let BoolFalseLength = 5 as ParsedInt

// "false" is the longest rendering, so 5 bytes cover both.
export let BoolToStringScratchBytes = 5 as ParsedInt

// The `.rdata` label prefixes for a literal's blob and its record. Distinct so a reader of the section
// can tell the two apart; `__`-prefixed so they never collide with a user name and stay out of the
// spec goldens (`TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction`'s data twin).
let StringBlobPrefix = "__str_blob_"
let StringRecordPrefix = "__str_rec_"

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO STREAM WRITERS — `stdlib/Print.maxon`'s and `stdlib/PrintError.maxon`'s ONLY floor (W35),
// and they REPLACE `__print_string`.** That entry point existed to serve a bare-name `print` BUILTIN,
// which is retired: `print` is now an ordinary call to the corpus declaration, whose body is
// `__Builtins.writeStdout(value.addressableBytes())`, and `printError` is its twin one stream over. So the
// graph did not go away — it gained a stderr sibling, a `__ManagedMemory` argument in place of a String
// pointer (the two records agree at `buffer@0`/`length@8`, which is all either reads) and a RESULT.
//
// `__`-prefixed (`isCompilerInternalCallee` treats them as the compiler's own — no E3004, no signature
// slotting), but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so `isRuntimeCallee` leaves them out of the heap-runtime decision.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THAT NARROWNESS IS A MEASURED POLICY, NOT AN OVERSIGHT — see `MmRuntime.isRuntimeCallee`.**
// Every W5-era runtime family declares `usesHeap` AND `usesManagedMemory` transitively, because its Std body calls
// `__managed_create`/`__mm_alloc` one tier down. These two bodies call NOTHING: two loads and one system op.
// Giving them a W5-shaped usage bit by reflex would drag the allocator and the leak gate into every
// program that writes a byte to a stream.
//
// ⚠ **`StreamWrite…`, NOT `WriteStderrName` — that spelling IS TAKEN, by the thing these must not be
// confused with.** `BacktraceFormat.WriteStderrName` is `mrt_write_cstr_stderr`, the hand-assembled
// NUL-terminated panic writer (see `StdOp.osWriteStderr` for why it cannot serve this). Naming a second
// constant that would be the same near-name trap as `X64Runtime.emitPrintString` against
// `buildPrintString`, one namespace collision closer.
export let StreamWriteStdoutName = b"__write_stdout"
export let StreamWriteStderrName = b"__write_stderr"
export let StrEqName = b"__str_eq"

// ⚠ **`StrCopyName` WENT TO `MmRuntime` WITH ITS BUILDER (W49 wave 9)** — a byte BLIT is a Std-tier
// operation, not a String's, and its consumers had already outgrown the name (`Parser`'s interpolation,
// `GtRuntime`'s subprocess buffers, `__mm_to_cstring`'s copy path). The emitted SYMBOL keeps its
// `__str_copy` spelling: a runtime callee's name is baked into the goldens and into `RuntimeUsage`'s bit,
// so renaming it would be a codegen change wearing a tidy-up's clothes.
//
// The decimal renderer an interpolation lowers CALLS to (`Parser.emitStringInterp`), which stays: it
// renders an integer part's text into a scratch buffer, and interpolation is this file's own subject.
// `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed — like
// `__str_eq` it touches no allocator, so it never widens the heap-runtime decision. That decision is
// driven by the interpolation's OWN `__mm_alloc` calls, which `scanRuntimeUsage` already sees.
export let IntToStringName = b"__int_to_string"

// The UNSIGNED decimal renderer (X11) — `__int_to_string` with no sign handling at all and an unsigned
// digit walk. It is a second ENTRY POINT and not a second implementation: one builder emits both (see
// `buildIntegerToString`), because the two graphs share their count loop and their write loop and
// differ only in the three places the sign reaches.
export let UIntToStringName = b"__uint_to_string"
export let BoolToStringName = b"__bool_to_string"

// ⭐ **WHICH DECIMAL RENDERER, AND WHAT SCRATCH IT NEEDS — the two halves of ONE choice.** The signed
// budget is one byte wider (the sign), so the dangerous pairing is the signed renderer with the unsigned
// budget: `i64.min` would write 21 bytes into a 20-byte scratch. Both are keyed off the same
// `IntegerSignedness` — the very enum the builder's own divides carry — and the one selection site
// (`Parser.materializeInterpExpr`) reads both off ONE local, so the wrong pairing cannot be reached by
// deciding twice.
//
// ⚠ It is not made UNREPRESENTABLE by the types, and saying so would be a claim the signatures do not
// keep: `integerToStringName(unsigned)` and `integerToStringScratchBytes(signed)` type-check. What rules
// it out is that no caller derives the signedness twice — so a SECOND selection site must take both from
// one decision too, or pass the `IntegerSignedness` itself rather than its two answers.
export function integerToStringName(signedness IntegerSignedness) returns ByteArray
	return match signedness 'reading'
		signed gives IntToStringName
		unsigned gives UIntToStringName
	end 'reading'
end 'integerToStringName'

export function integerToStringScratchBytes(signedness IntegerSignedness) returns ParsedInt
	return match signedness 'reading'
		signed gives IntToStringScratchBytes
		unsigned gives UIntToStringScratchBytes
	end 'reading'
end 'integerToStringScratchBytes'

// The surviving Wave-D entry point. `__str_decref(record)` is a String's drop — a `__mm_decref` that ALSO
// frees the external buffer of a detached String. It is `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no
// signature slotting) but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed, so on its own it does not widen the heap decision. It DOES
// call `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free` inside its Std body — which `scanRuntimeUsage` cannot see, since those
// calls are not in the Maxon module — so a reference to it explicitly turns on `usesHeap`.
//
// ⚠ **`__str_append` STOOD BESIDE IT AND RETIRED WITH `String.append` (W49 wave 8).** The corpus body
// appends through `managed.append(other.managed)`, which is `__managed_append` over the receiver's own record,
// so the String-specific grower had no producer left. Its two behaviours survive in the corpus rather than
// being lost: the `singleByteGraphemesFlag` maintenance is the body's own `if`, and the GEOMETRIC growth it
// spelled as `2 * requiredLen` is `__managed_grown_cap`'s, which `__managed_append` reaches through
// `__managed_reserve`. That second one is load-bearing — exact growth made `var s = ""` plus a loop of appends
// quadratic, MEASURED at ×3.94 ×4.07 ×3.82 ×3.88 per doubling — so a retirement onto a runtime with no
// growth policy would have restored the quadratic silently.
export let StrDecrefName = b"__str_decref"

// ⭐⭐ **THE P1.8 SLICE C BYTE/ASCII METHOD FAMILY IS GONE — ALL EIGHT ENTRY POINTS, ALL SEVEN MEMBERS**
// (W49 waves 1, 2, 4 and 5). It was `__str_starts_with`/`__str_ends_with` (wave 1), `__str_map_case`,
// `__str_replace` and `__str_replace_first` (wave 2), `__str_contains` (wave 4), and finally
// `__str_split` — plus the two it was the last consumer of, `__str_find` and `__str_range_eq` — at wave 5.
// Every one of the seven members is now an ordinary declared function in `stdlib/String.maxon`, reached
// through `Parser.memberBelongsToTheCorpus`, and the corpus bodies these entry points had been transcribed
// FROM are what serves them.
//
// ⚠ **`__str_find` AND `__str_range_eq` DID NOT GO HOME — THEY DIED, AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT FACT.** A
// retired MEMBER hands its body back to the corpus; these two were never members, they were the search
// underneath three of them. `contains` (wave 4) and `__strix_find` (wave 3) had already gone, which left
// `__str_split` as `__str_find`'s only producer and `__str_find` as `__str_range_eq`'s only producer — so
// with `split` retired, nothing in the compiler can emit either callee and no `RuntimeUsage` bit could ever
// turn true. What replaces them is the corpus's own `findIn`/`bytesEqual` (`stdlib/String.maxon:998`,
// `:976`), which these two were transcribed from in the first place. **THE ORDER MATTERS AND IS THE TRAP
// THIS FAMILY KEEPS SETTING**: an entry point is deleted only once EVERY producer of its callee is gone,
// never when merely its most visible one is (see `closeStringMethodNeeds`, whose clauses shed TERMS one at
// a time for exactly this reason, and the paragraph `StdlibSource` keeps where its synthesized-runtime seed
// stood, which states the same rule for a REACHABILITY term — that seed shed its last one at W129).
//
// ⚠ **THE COST MOVED WITH THE BODY, AND IT IS ACCEPTED** (user ruling, recorded at wave 2). The corpus
// `findIn` asks `byteAt` — a THROWING, bounds-checked accessor — once per candidate position, where
// `__str_find` walked the bytes with a raw load and a first-byte fast reject in one Std body; and the
// corpus `sliceBytes` builds each segment through `managed.slice` + a `String{…}` literal, where
// `__str_split` blitted it straight into a fused record. shv2 has no inliner, so both differences are real
// calls per byte and per segment. Two candidate cures were MEASURED and both are regressions; do not
// attempt a third here (the full argument is at `Parser.stringSurfaceMemberNames`).

// ⚠ **`__str_map_case`, `__str_replace` AND `__str_replace_first` WERE HERE AND WENT WITH THEIR BUILDERS**
// (W49 wave 2). `toLower`/`toUpper`/`replace`/`replaceFirst` are the CORPUS's now — struck from
// `Parser.stringSurfaceMemberNames` — so nothing names any of the three and a constant for them could only
// ever spell a callee no module emits. The corpus bodies behind them reach `byteAt`, `setByte`,
// `addressableBytes` and `__ManagedMemory.slice`, whose doors are all still shv2's.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`__str_from_bytes(arr) -> String` — `__str_of_buffer`'s TWIN, TAKEN WHEN THE FLAG MUST BE SCANNED
// RATHER THAN SUPPLIED.** It is `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) and not
// `__mm_`-prefixed, so it declares its own transitive floor in `closeStringMethodNeeds`.
//
// ⛔ **IT IS NO LONGER `String.from(bytes)`'S BODY, AND THIS HEADER SAID SO UNTIL W55 — WHICH IS EXACTLY
// THE LIFETIME OF THE CLAIM.** W17 listed `stdlib/String.maxon`, so `from(bytes)` is the corpus's own
// declaration and W55 retired the static roster that intercepted it. What reaches this graph now is
// `Parser.emitFusedWrapperLiteral`'s no-flag arm — `Character{managed: X}`, whose declaration carries no
// `singleByteGraphemesFlag` field for a caller to pass — so it is LIVE and load-bearing (`c.clone()` over a
// `Character` emits `x64.callDirect __str_from_bytes`, measured), just not for the member it was built for.
// The flag-carrying arm, which is every corpus `String{…}`, goes to `__str_of_buffer`.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH COPY, WHICH IS WHY RETIRING THE STATIC MOVED NO OWNERSHIP.** The ARGUMENT for copying is stated
// once, at `StrOfBufferName` — the view is representable since the P1.7 COW slice and is declined on
// failure-mode grounds, not on impossibility. Do not restate it here and do not restate its retracted
// predecessor ("shv2 CANNOT share"), which is what this header used to point at.
export let StrFromBytesName = b"__str_from_bytes"

// ⭐⭐ **`__str_of_buffer(srcRecord, singleByteGraphemes) -> String` — THE ENVELOPE COLLAPSE'S CONSTRUCTOR:
// what a corpus `String{managed: X, singleByteGraphemesFlag: F}` LOWERS TO.** `type String` declares two
// fields and IS the fused record (`Project.StructLayout.isInlineManagedField`), so its literal cannot
// store a pointer at offset 0 the way an ordinary struct literal stores a field — offset 0 is `buffer@0`
// of the record being built. This is the producer that builds it instead.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS `__str_from_bytes` WITH THE FLAG SUPPLIED RATHER THAN SCANNED, AND IT IS THE SAME GRAPH
// UNDERNEATH** (`emitBuildOwnedInlineString`). The two differ in exactly one thing and it is the one thing
// the corpus is explicit about: `String.from(bytes)` classifies the bytes it was handed, while
// `fromOwnedBytes` takes a caller *"that ALREADY KNOWS how the bytes classify"* — `StringBuilder.build`
// watched every byte go in, so re-scanning them *"would make finishing a builder cost a second walk"*
// (`stdlib/String.maxon:139-142`). Sharing the builder is what keeps the two from drifting into two record
// shapes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT COPIES, WHERE THE REFERENCE BUILDS A ZERO-COPY VIEW — DELIBERATELY, AND THIS IS A DIFFERENT
// ARGUMENT FROM THE ONE `buildStrFromBytes` USED TO MAKE.** That function's header says shv2 *cannot* share,
// *"has no shared-ownership relationship between an `Array` record and a `String` record"*; that was true
// when written and the P1.7 COW slice made it false — `buildStrBytesView`, two screens down, establishes
// exactly that relationship in the other direction, increfing the allocation the bytes live in and stating
// its refcount correct in both drop orders. **So the view is now REPRESENTABLE.** It is not taken here
// because a view is the shape whose failure mode is a double free or a read of returned memory, and it
// buys a constant factor on a path nothing has measured. Copying is what every other producer in this file
// hands back and what `__str_decref` already reclaims. ⇒ A rung with the ladder read, not a line here.
export let StrOfBufferName = b"__str_of_buffer"

// ⭐⭐ **`__mm_to_cstring(bufRecord) -> ptr` — `__ManagedMemory.toCString()`, the corpus's ONE producer of a
// `cstring`** (`stdlib/String.maxon`'s `cstr()`). It implements the contract that declaration states: the
// answer is always safely NUL-terminated, even when the underlying `__ManagedMemory` is packed tight
// against its allocated capacity — which is exactly where `strlen` would otherwise walk past the end into
// adjacent heap.
//
// ⭐ **THE CHECK IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION, IT IS WHAT MAKES THE COMMON CASE FREE.** Every String literal's
// blob is NUL-terminated by `stringBlobBytes`, and every owned inline String's trailing NUL comes from the
// slab's zeroing contract — so `buffer[length]` is already 0 for both, the test falls through, and the
// receiver's own bytes are handed back untouched. Only a buffer that is neither pays a grow.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND THE CHECK MAY NOT BE ASKED OF EVERY RECORD, WHICH IS A SEPARATE FACT FROM WHICH ONES ANSWER
// YES.** `buffer[length]` is one byte PAST the content; a VIEW and an exactly-full OWNED buffer do not own
// it, and a zero found there is one nothing keeps zero. `buildMmToCString` routes on
// `BufferOwnership.emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot` before the load, and that emitter's header carries the
// whole rule — including the C# bootstrap's `LowerManagedToCString`, which states it in the other language
// and must move with it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **NOTHING IS ALLOCATED ON EITHER PATH, AND THAT IS WHY A `cstring` NEEDS NO OWNER.** A `cstring` is a
// raw pointer with no length, no capacity and no refcount — the type exists precisely to hand a
// `__Builtins.*` intrinsic something to `strlen` — so there is no owner to record it against and no drop
// that could reclaim it, in this compiler or in either reference. ⇒ The pointer this answers is always the
// RECEIVER'S OWN BUFFER: either it already ended in a NUL, or the record was grown by one byte and
// terminated in place. The record keeps it, the record frees it, and the caller's whole obligation is to
// keep the receiver ALIVE and UNMUTATED for as long as it holds the pointer.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO COPY INTO A FRESH `__mm_alloc` BLOCK ON THE SECOND PATH, AND THAT BLOCK WAS OWNED BY
// NOBODY — exit 101 ON FIVE LINES OF ORDINARY MAXON.** `var s = ""` + `s.append("hello world")` +
// `s.cstr()` leaves `capacity == length`, which the terminator-slot router (rightly) refuses to probe, so
// the receiver took the copy and leaked it. The old text argued the leak was the DECLARATION's to fix and
// that nothing reached it anyway — *"`cstr()` has no caller, so dead-function elimination drops both"* —
// and BOTH halves were false: `stdlib/String.maxon`'s `cstr()` is `public`, `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` calls
// it at seven sites, and an ordinary user program reaches it (`toCString()` is refused outside `stdlib/` by
// `Parser.requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod`, but `cstr()` is the corpus's own public forwarder, so that gate
// is on the SPELLING and never was one on reachability).
//
// ⭐ **THE COW-AND-GROW SHAPE IS BOTH REFERENCES' OWN**, which is the strongest evidence available that it
// is the right one: the C# bootstrap's `MaxonToStandardConversion.LowerManagedToCString` grows through
// `maxon_string_ensure_cap`, and v1's `stdlib/Internals.maxon:__managed_mem_to_cstring` through
// `__managed_mem_grow`. shv2 spells the same step `__managed_reserve`.
export let MmToCStringName = b"__mm_to_cstring"

// ⭐⭐ **`__mm_from_cstring(cstr) -> arrRecord` — `__ManagedMemory.fromCString(cstr)`, AND IT IS
// `__mm_to_cstring`'s EXACT DUAL.** One walks a record's bytes out to a NUL-terminated pointer; this walks
// a NUL-terminated pointer back into a fresh byte-element `Array` record. They live beside each other for
// the reason `ManagedDirectoryRuntime`'s load/store byte pair does: a representation's reader and its
// writer are ONE fact, and the pair this project keeps finding drifted is the one that was split up.
//
// ⭐ **IT ALLOCATES AND COPIES, AND THAT IS THE ONLY ANSWER THAT TYPES.** A `cstring` carries no length, no
// capacity and no ownership; a `__ManagedMemory` is a real `Array` record whose `length@8` and `buffer@0`
// every consumer reads. There is no view to take — the source is a bare address whose lifetime this
// function cannot see — so the bytes are measured with a `strlen` walk and blitted into a record the CALLER
// owns. That is v1's shape too (`stdlib/Internals.maxon:4341`'s `__managed_mem_from_cstring`).
//
// ⚠ **THE WHOLE BODY IS `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitCStringToManaged`, WHICH ALREADY EXISTED FOR
// `filename()` AND `currentPath()`.** This door is a THIRD caller of that builder, not a third copy of the
// loop; what it adds is a Maxon-reachable name for a conversion the runtime had been doing for the OS
// families only. See that builder for the strlen/copy shape and for why the record's `ValueId` is minted by
// the caller.
export let MmFromCStringName = b"__mm_from_cstring"

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE BYTE DOOR LEFT — what a FUSED WRAPPER's inline `managed` read mints.**
//
//   * `__str_bytes_view(str) -> arrRecord` — a ZERO-COPY `Array with Byte` VIEW over the receiver's live
//     bytes: nothing is copied and the cost does not depend on the length. See `buildStrBytesView` for why
//     it is a fresh record rather than the String pointer retagged.
//
// ⚠⚠ **ITS PRODUCER MOVED AT W49 WAVE 7 AND THE DOOR DID NOT DIE WITH IT.** `String.addressableBytes()` was a
// synthesized arm minting this callee; the member retired onto `stdlib/String.maxon:181`, whose entire body
// is `return managed` — and `Parser.emitFieldLoad`'s inline-managed arm lowers exactly that to exactly this.
// So the corpus body IS the arm, one frame down, and the entry point has a live producer for the same reason
// it always did. (The `addressableBytes` name no longer appears in this compiler at all, which is why this
// header describes the LOWERING rather than the member.)
//
// ⛔⛔ **`__str_byte_at` AND `__str_byte_at_or_panic` STOOD HERE AND ARE GONE (W49 wave 7).** They were
// `byteAt(index)` and `byteAtOrPanic(index)` — one bounds-checked read with two failure exits, built by one
// `buildStrByteRead` — and both members retired onto the corpus (`stdlib/String.maxon:284`, `:299`), whose
// bodies forward to `__ManagedMemory.byteAt`. With the two arms gone nothing in this compiler can emit
// either callee, so `usesStrByteAt`/`usesStrByteAtOrPanic` could never turn true, `buildStrByteRead` could
// never run, and `isThrowingStringRuntimeCallee` — whose ONE term was `__str_byte_at` — could never answer
// `true`. All four went in one edit, which is the order this family's own rule demands: an entry point dies
// only once EVERY producer of its callee is gone.
//
// ⭐ **AND ONE ABORT DISSOLVED WITH THEM RATHER THAN MOVING.** `RuntimeAbort.stringByteAtOutOfRange` existed
// because the panicking read was a SYNTHESIZED function with *"no source line and no message to bake"*, so
// it could not reach `osPanic` and reported a number instead. The corpus's `byteAtOrPanic` is
// `try byteAt(index) otherwise panic("String.byteAtOrPanic: index out of bounds …")` — ordinary Maxon with a
// real line and a real message — so that failure now prints the reference's own text on every target. The
// abort's whole justification was the absence this retirement removed.
//
// `__str_bytes_view` is `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) and not
// `__mm_`-prefixed, so it declares its own transitive floor in `closeStringMethodNeeds`.
export let StrBytesViewName = b"__str_bytes_view"

// ⚠ **`StrFindNotFound` WAS HERE AND WENT WITH `__str_find`** (W49 wave 5). It was that search's "no
// occurrence" answer — a NEGATIVE INDEX rather than a thrown error, because its callers were not throwing
// and a `tryCall` per iteration would have been a control-flow join to answer a question they treated as
// ordinary. The corpus spells the same search `throws StringError.notFound` (`findIn`), which is the
// reference's own shape and needs no sentinel. `CommandLineRuntime.NoMoreArguments` is the one negative
// byte-position sentinel left, and it now carries the argument itself rather than citing this one.

// `__str_clone(src) -> newStr` — a String's DEEP CLONE (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b): a fresh OWNED heap String, an
// independent copy of `src` that is droppable through `__str_decref` without touching `src`. The `(box) ->
// newBox` DUAL of `__str_decref`, so a managed array/struct that clones a String field through this drops it
// through `__str_decref`. `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) but NOT
// `__mm_`-prefixed; it calls `__mm_alloc`/`__str_copy` inside its Std body, which `scanRuntimeUsage` cannot
// see, so a reference to it turns on `usesHeap`/`usesStrCopy` explicitly (`recordCallUsage`).
export let StrCloneName = b"__str_clone"

// ⭐⭐ **`__str_retain(record) -> ownedRecord` — BECOME AN OWNER OF A BORROWED BYTE RECORD WITHOUT KNOWING
// WHICH KIND IT IS.** It is `__str_clone` when the record is an IMMORTAL `.rdata` literal and `__mm_incref`
// when it is a heap record, and either way the caller owns exactly one reference it must `__str_decref`.
//
// ⭐ **THE CHOICE IS DEFERRED TO RUN TIME BECAUSE NO COMPILE-TIME TIER CAN MAKE IT.** A borrowed `String`
// PARAMETER is a heap record when its caller owned one and an immortal `.rdata` record when its caller
// wrote a literal — `pick("ab", c: true)` and `pick(v, c: true)` over one `pick(s String, c bool)` differ
// in nothing the callee's frame can read. Getting it wrong is a fault in one direction and a lost write in
// the other: `__mm_incref` on `.rdata` writes a refcount into a read-only image section, and a `__str_clone`
// of a heap record hands the caller a COPY, so an append through it never reaches the record's owner
// (MEASURED, both: `grow(s if c else "lit")` was 0xC0000005 and `grow(make() if c else s)` printed `v=ab`
// where the oracle prints `v=abXY`).
//
// ⭐ **AND THE RECORD ALREADY CARRIES THE ANSWER** — `capacity@16 = ImmortalRecordCapacity` is the literal
// provenance `BufferOwnership` declares, so the test is one load and one compare. This is
// `__retain_existential` one tier down: the same three-way protocol choice `promoteBorrowedToOwned` makes
// statically, deferred to the one party that can make it.
//
// ⚠ **IT MUST BE READ FOR ITS RESULT, NEVER SUBSTITUTED BY ITS ARGUMENT.** The incref arm hands back the
// record it was given; the clone arm hands back a DIFFERENT one. A caller that dropped the result and used
// the source would write through the immortal record it was called to avoid.
//
// `__`-prefixed compiler-internal (no E3004, no signature slotting) but NOT `__mm_`-prefixed; its two arms
// call `__str_clone` and `__mm_incref` inside its Std body, which `scanRuntimeUsage` cannot see, so a
// reference declares those needs explicitly (`recordCallUsage`).
export let StrRetainName = b"__str_retain"

// ⭐ **WHERE A STRING RECORD KEEPS THE BUFFER-OWNERSHIP ENCODING** (`BufferOwnership`) — the three slots
// its predicates read, built from THIS file's offsets. All three provenances the record format admits are
// ordinary values of that encoding, and no String-only branch stands in front of it:
//
//   * INLINE (`capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`, `parent@32 = NoOwedAllocation`) — every owned String
//     is born this way (`Parser.emitStringInterp`, which `promoteToOwnedString` also routes through). Not
//     mine to write in place, and no separate allocation owed: the record's own free takes the bytes.
//   * DETACHED (`capacity@16 >= 0`) — a separate `__mm_alloc`'d buffer this record owns and must release,
//     which is exactly what the encoding calls an OWNER, so its base is its own `buffer@0`.
//   * IMMORTAL (`capacity@16 = ImmortalRecordCapacity`) — a literal's immortal blob, in a record that is
//     immortal too. It never reaches these two functions: the parser promotes a `var` String at its
//     declaration and only an owned String is ever dropped.
//     ⛔⛔ **AND "IT NEEDS NO ARM OF ITS OWN REGARDLESS" STOOD HERE AND IS WITHDRAWN (W157 review).** The
//     argument was that an immortal record is a non-owned buffer owing nothing, which the encoding already
//     says — true of the BUFFER half, and silent about the RECORD. `__str_decref` reaching one would run the
//     shared refcount check against a header that is `.rdata` and then `__mm_free` an image address; that is
//     why `__managed_decref` grew an explicit `emitRecordIsImmortal` arm at W157, the moment
//     `addressableBytes()` made an immortal record reachable by a drop path at all. ⇒ **what keeps this
//     function safe is the REACHABILITY argument above and nothing else**, so a change that lets a literal
//     be dropped owes `__str_decref` the same arm its twin already has.
//
// ⚠ **THIS REPLACES `parent@32 == -1` ("a detached root"), AND THE BEHAVIOUR IT DECIDED IS UNCHANGED.**
// That test was this file's own third spelling of "is this buffer mine?" — and, at the same offset as the
// Array record's `parent@32`, it meant the OPPOSITE thing (there, *someone else's allocation I owe a
// release to*). Now `parent@32` means one thing in both records and nothing here has to be told which.
// True iff every GRAPHEME of these bytes is exactly one byte — the `singleByteGraphemes@48` scan, run once
// per distinct literal at lower time (the cold path), never per value.
//
// ⭐ **EXPORTED BECAUSE THE PARSER ASKS THE SAME QUESTION OF THE SAME BYTES.** `Parser.foldSliceFromStart`
// folds `lit.slice(lit.startIndex(), length: n)` into a literal of its own only when a GRAPHEME count and a
// BYTE count are the same number for those bytes — which is this predicate, and re-deriving it there would be
// two spellings of one rule, free to disagree about `` on the day one of them was edited.
//
// ⭐ **ONE PASS TESTING BOTH, rather than an ASCII scan followed by a CR scan** — the corpus's
// `scanSingleByteGraphemes` (`stdlib/String.maxon:1066`) is the same loop for the same stated reason, and
// this is its compile-time twin over a literal's bytes.
export function bytesAreSingleByteGraphemes(bytes ByteArray) returns bool
	for b in bytes 'each'
		// A CR is ASCII and is NOT a grapheme on its own — GB3 joins it to a following LF — so it is the one
		// byte that clears this flag without being high.
		if b >= AsciiHighBit or b == CarriageReturnByte 'notSingleByte'
			return false
		end 'notSingleByte'
	end 'each'
	return true
end 'bytesAreSingleByteGraphemes'

// The NUL-terminated blob for a literal's payload — the bytes, then one NUL.
//
// ⛔ **IT DOES NOT PAD, AND IT USED TO.** The pad to 8 lived here, and its comment claimed the
// consequence for the WHOLE `.rdata` section — that a record registering right after a blob therefore
// lands 8-aligned — which was true only for as long as no other producer registered an odd-length
// payload. `installSlabRuntime`'s 129- and 249-byte class tables did exactly that (378 ≡ 2 mod 8), and
// from that point every String record `installEnumLookupHelpers` registered was misaligned, which on
// arm64-macOS is an unencodable dyld chained-fixup rebase delta and a compiler panic. Alignment is now
// `GlobalDataTable.RdataPayloadAlignment`, applied by `appendRdataPayload` to every payload of every
// kind — the one writer of a slot's offset, which is the only place that can promise it.
function stringBlobBytes(bytes ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	var blob = ByteArray.create()
	blob.append(bytes)
	blob.push(NulTerminator)
	return blob
end 'stringBlobBytes'

// Build the 56-byte record for a literal. `byteLength` EXCLUDES the NUL (it is the payload count);
// `singleByteGraphemes` is `bytesAreSingleByteGraphemes`'s answer.
//
// ⭐ **`capacity@16` IS `ImmortalRecordCapacity`, NOT `RdataBufferCapacity`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THIS
// FUNCTION** (W157). Both say "the buffer is an immortal `.rdata` blob"; only this one also says "and so is
// the record you are holding" — which is exactly what a record ASSEMBLED AS BYTES AND REGISTERED AS AN
// `.rdata` CONSTANT is, and what a byte-string literal's heap `__mm_alloc` box is not. Since W157 hands a
// String's own record out as a buffer (`buildStrBytesView`), that difference is what stops `__mm_incref` and
// `__managed_decref` performing a refcount read-modify-write on read-only memory.
//
// ⚠ **`buffer@0` HOLDS THE BLOB'S RDATA OFFSET, NOT 0, AND THIS IS LOAD-BEARING FOR DEDUP.** The PE
// writer OVERWRITES `buffer@0` with the blob's absolute VA (`ImageBase + rdataRva + blobOffset`), so
// the value stored here is irrelevant to what the program reads. But it is NOT irrelevant to
// `registerRdataConstant`, which DEDUPES records by their bytes: two different strings of the same
// length and ascii-ness have byte-identical records EXCEPT for `buffer@0`, so a placeholder of 0 would
// collapse `"hello"` and `"world"` into ONE record pointing at ONE blob — a silent wrong answer where
// every same-length string compares equal. Seeding `buffer@0` with the blob's own offset (distinct per
// distinct blob, shared by identical strings that already dedup their blob) makes records dedup EXACTLY
// when their strings are identical.
//
// ⭐ EACH FIELD IS APPENDED AT ITS NAMED OFFSET AND THE APPENDER ASSERTS IT LANDS THERE, so the offsets
// the runtime functions load by (`StringBufferOffset`, `StringLengthOffset`) and the offsets this
// writes are ONE set of constants that cannot drift: reorder a field or mis-size one and this panics
// rather than shipping a record the runtime reads wrong.
// ⚠ **NOT `export`ED SINCE W49 WAVE 9** — the same orphan sweep. `registerStringRecord` is its one caller
// and the door every other file comes through; an `export` here advertised a second way to assemble the
// record that would skip the blob registration the offset seed depends on.
function buildStringRecordBytes(blobOffset ByteOffset, byteLength BytePos, singleByteGraphemes bool) returns ByteArray
	var record = ByteArray.create()
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringBufferOffset, value: blobOffset as ParsedInt)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringLengthOffset, value: byteLength as ParsedInt)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringCapacityOffset, value: ImmortalRecordCapacity)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringElementSizeOffset, value: ByteStringElementSize)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringParentPtrOffset, value: NoOwedAllocation)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringElementDestroyOffset, value: TrivialElementDestroy)
	appendRecordField(record, expectedOffset: StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset, value: singleByteGraphemesFlagValue(singleByteGraphemes))

	if record.count() != StringRecordBytes 'sizeDrift'
		panic("buildStringRecordBytes: assembled {record.count()} bytes, not {StringRecordBytes} — a field's width does not match its offset spacing")
	end 'sizeDrift'
	return record
end 'buildStringRecordBytes'

function appendRecordField(record ByteArray, expectedOffset ByteOffset, value ParsedInt)
	if record.count() != expectedOffset 'offsetDrift'
		panic("buildStringRecordBytes: a record field is being written at offset {record.count()} but its constant says {expectedOffset} — the record layout the runtime reads and this writes have drifted")
	end 'offsetDrift'
	record.append(scalarImage(value, byteCount: StringFieldBytes))
end 'appendRecordField'

function singleByteGraphemesFlagValue(singleByteGraphemes bool) returns ParsedInt
	if singleByteGraphemes 'oneByteEach'
		return SingleByteGraphemesTrue
	end 'oneByteEach'
	return SingleByteGraphemesFalse
end 'singleByteGraphemesFlagValue'

// The `.rdata` labels a literal's blob and record register under. Split out so the two prefixes are
// named at the one place that uses them and `LowerMaxonToStd` names neither.
export function registerStringBlob(globalData GlobalDataTable, bytes ByteArray) returns String
	return globalData.registerRdataConstant(StringBlobPrefix, bytes: stringBlobBytes(bytes), kind: RdataPayloadKind.stringBlob)
end 'registerStringBlob'

// Register a literal's record, seeded with its blob's offset (see `buildStringRecordBytes` for why the
// offset, not 0). `blobLabel` must already be registered — its offset is read back here.
function registerStringRecord(globalData GlobalDataTable, blobLabel String, byteLength BytePos, singleByteGraphemes bool) returns String
	let blobOffset = globalData.rdataOffsetOf(blobLabel)
	return globalData.registerRdataConstant(StringRecordPrefix, bytes: buildStringRecordBytes(blobOffset, byteLength: byteLength, singleByteGraphemes: singleByteGraphemes), kind: RdataPayloadKind.stringRecord)
end 'registerStringRecord'

// ⭐ **THE WHOLE OF "MAKE THESE BYTES AN IMMORTAL `.rdata` String", AS ONE CALL — blob, record, and the
// fixup that points the record's `buffer@0` at the blob's final address.** The three steps are ONE act: a
// record registered without the fixup carries the blob's rdata OFFSET where a pointer belongs
// (`buildStringRecordBytes` seeds it deliberately, for dedup), so a caller that forgot the third line would
// ship a String whose buffer address is a small integer.
//
// ⚠ **TWO CALLERS WITH NOTHING ELSE IN COMMON, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A DOOR AND NOT A LOCAL SEQUENCE**:
// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerStringLiteral`, which turns a source `"…"` into a value, and
// `EnumLookupRuntime.synthesizeEnumFromName`, which needs one constant String per case NAME inside a body it
// builds after the pipeline. The string a literal produces and the string that helper compares against are
// then one shaping — which is the whole point, because they are compared with each other by `__str_eq`.
export function registerStringLiteralRecord(globalData GlobalDataTable, bytes ByteArray) returns String
	let blobLabel = registerStringBlob(globalData, bytes: bytes)
	let recordLabel = registerStringRecord(globalData, blobLabel: blobLabel, byteLength: bytes.count(), singleByteGraphemes: bytesAreSingleByteGraphemes(bytes))
	globalData.recordStringBufferFixup(recordLabel, blobLabel: blobLabel)
	return recordLabel
end 'registerStringLiteralRecord'

// ⚠⚠ **THE `RuntimeUsage` MACHINERY STOOD HERE — 760 LINES OF IT — AND IS `Runtime/RuntimeUsage.maxon`
// NOW (W49 wave 9).** `RuntimeUsage` itself, `scanRuntimeUsage`, `recordCallUsage` and the four
// `record*Usage`/`close*Needs` pairs decided the install of the green-thread scheduler, the IOCP
// substrate, the streaming subprocess API, the array, set, map and list runtimes, the two descriptor
// drops, the incref/retain helpers, the union destructors, the struct cloners, the wall clock, managed
// files and directories, the command line, standard input and process introspection — none of which is a
// String. A String was ONE TENANT of that list and never its landlord, and a file named for the tenant
// told every later reader that a new family's bit belonged in a String file.
//
// What is below this line is what the name has always claimed: the record a String IS, the literal that
// materializes it, and the graphs behind `==`, drop, clone and the two byte doors.

// Install the referenced String runtime functions into the lowered Std module. Called AFTER the
// pipeline (like `installMmRuntime`), so these are appended already-lowered — what runs over that appended
// band is stated once, in `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s install block.
// Each is installed ONLY when referenced — vocabulary does not ship ahead of its consumer.
export function installStringRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesWriteStdout 'writestdout'
		buildStreamWrite(module, name: StreamWriteStdoutName, stream: OsOutputStream.standardOutput)
	end 'writestdout'
	if usage.usesWriteStderr 'writestderr'
		buildStreamWrite(module, name: StreamWriteStderrName, stream: OsOutputStream.standardError)
	end 'writestderr'
	if usage.usesStrEq 'streq'
		buildStrEq(module, name: StrEqName)
	end 'streq'
	// ⚠ **THE BUILDER IS `MmRuntime`'s NOW AND THE INSTALL ARM DELIBERATELY IS NOT (W49 wave 9).** A byte
	// blit is a Std-tier operation, so the graph moved; the ARM stays here because moving it into
	// `installMmRuntime` would change two things at once. That installer is gated on `usesHeap`, and this
	// bit is not a subset of it (`GtRuntime`'s subprocess buffers blit without the program allocating) —
	// and the install ORDER is the Std module's FUNCTION order, which is emitted code. A relocation that
	// claims codegen neutrality does not get to move a symbol's position as a side effect.
	if usage.usesStrCopy 'strcopy'
		buildStrCopy(module)
	end 'strcopy'
	if usage.usesIntToString 'inttostr'
		buildIntegerToString(module, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed)
	end 'inttostr'
	if usage.usesUIntToString 'uinttostr'
		buildIntegerToString(module, signedness: IntegerSignedness.unsigned)
	end 'uinttostr'
	if usage.usesBoolToString 'booltostr'
		buildBoolToString(module)
	end 'booltostr'
	if usage.usesStrDecref 'strdecref'
		buildStrDecref(module)
	end 'strdecref'
	if usage.usesStrClone 'strclone'
		buildStrClone(module)
	end 'strclone'
	if usage.usesStrRetain 'strretain'
		buildStrRetain(module)
	end 'strretain'
	if usage.usesStrFromBytes 'strfrombytes'
		buildStrFromBytes(module)
	end 'strfrombytes'
	if usage.usesStrOfBuffer 'strofbuffer'
		buildStrOfBuffer(module)
	end 'strofbuffer'
	if usage.usesMmToCString 'mmtocstring'
		buildMmToCString(module)
	end 'mmtocstring'
	if usage.usesMmFromCString 'mmfromcstring'
		buildMmFromCString(module)
	end 'mmfromcstring'
	if usage.usesStrBytesView 'strbytesview'
		buildStrBytesView(module)
	end 'strbytesview'
end 'installStringRuntime'

// `__write_stdout(mm)` / `__write_stderr(mm)` — write a `__ManagedMemory`'s bytes to a standard stream, no
// trailing newline, answering the byte count the OS reports.
//
// It loads `buffer@0` and `length@8` from the record and hands them to the stream's system op, which lowers
// per target — x64-windows to `GetStdHandle` + `WriteFile` through the IAT, arm64-macOS to a POSIX `write`
// through the GOT, wasm to a `wasi:cli` accessor plus `blocking-write-and-flush`. The op is target-neutral,
// so this function names no OS symbol AND sets no frame size: `outgoingArgSize` (WriteFile's stack-arg space
// on x64, ZERO on arm64's all-register `write`) is a TARGET ABI fact, so each backend's isel sets it from the
// op it actually lowers. Setting it here would bake an x64 frame size into a target-neutral function — the
// leak that made the arm64 prologue panic.
//
// ⭐ **PARAMETERIZED so ONE graph serves TWO symbols**, exactly as `buildStrEq` serves `__str_eq` and the
// `Equatable` witness: the stream is the only difference, and the record read is not a fact worth writing
// twice.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENT IS A `__ManagedMemory`, NOT A String — and the offsets are the same ones because a
// String record IS one.** `stdlib/Print.maxon` hands over `value.addressableBytes()`, which since W157 is
// the receiver's own record rather than a minted view; either way the two shapes agree on
// `buffer@0`/`length@8`, which is all this reads. The bytes are BORROWED: this reads through them and
// never frees, so a literal argument needs no decref.
function buildStreamWrite(module StdModule, name ByteArray, stream OsOutputStream)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0 as ValueId

	let buffer = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(buffer, addrId: record, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let length = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(length, addrId: record, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let written = ids.mint()
	match stream 's'
		standardOutput then module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osWriteStdout(written, bufId: buffer, lenId: length))
		standardError then module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osWriteStderr(written, bufId: buffer, lenId: length))
	end 's'

	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(written))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStreamWrite'

// `<name>(a, b) -> i64(bool)` — content equality: equal LENGTHS and equal BYTES. BOTH String operands are
// BORROWED (read through, never freed), so this allocation-free body is the equality both the `==` operator
// and the `Equatable` witness need.
//
// ⭐ **PARAMETERIZED BY NAME so ONE graph serves TWO symbols.** It is emitted under `__str_eq` for the `==`
// operator path (`installStringRuntime`) AND under `String.equals` for the `Equatable` witness path
// (`installBuiltinConformanceRuntime`), because the witness reloc names the impl `String.equals`
// (`qualifiedMethodName("String", "equals")`), NOT `__str_eq`. Same byte-compare, two `.text` symbols — the
// `buildIntEquals` pattern (which builds `int.equals` fresh), so the two paths cannot drift a byte apart.
//
// This is the bootstrap's `bytesEqual` (a length check, then a byte loop), NOT the enum `memcmp`
// helper. It allocates nothing. Empty strings: length 0 == length 0 → the loop runs zero times →
// return 1. The byte loop itself is `emitByteRangeCompareLoop` — shared with `__managed_eq`; see that emitter
// for why it is a shared EMITTER and not a shared CALL.
// ⭐ **IT RETURNS `bool`, NOT A MACHINE WORD, AND THE WITNESS SLOT IS WHY** (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-B). Under the
// `String.equals` name this graph lands in an `Equatable` witness table, whose slot every dispatch site types
// from `Equatable.equals(other Self) returns bool` — so its declared result must be the one
// `builtinProtocolResultStdType` gives that declaration, an `i1`. Declared `i64` it agreed on the register
// targets (a `bool` rides a 64-bit register) and trapped `indirect call type mismatch` on wasm. The `__str_eq`
// name shares the declaration for the reason it shares the body: ONE graph cannot have two signatures, and
// the operator path already types its result `boolean` at the call site (`Parser.emitStringEquality`), so
// this is the type it was always claiming to return.
export function buildStrEq(module StdModule, name ByteArray)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(builtinProtocolResultStdType(BoolConformerName)), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var notEqual = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notEqual")
	var lenMatch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lenMatch")
	var allMatch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allMatch")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let a = 0 as ValueId
	let b = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: lengths differ ⇒ not equal; else fall into the byte loop.
	let aLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: a, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let bLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: b, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let lenEq = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: aLen, b: bLen, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(lenEq, thenBlockId: lenMatch.id, elseBlockId: notEqual.id))

	// lenMatch: the buffers, then the shared byte loop over `aLen` bytes of each.
	let aBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: lenMatch, record: a, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let bBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: lenMatch, record: b, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	emitByteRangeCompareLoop(module, func: func, cur: lenMatch, aBuf: aBuf, bBuf: bBuf, len: aLen, allMatch: allMatch, notEqual: notEqual, ids: ids)

	emitConstReturn(module, block: allMatch, value: PredicateTrue, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: notEqual, value: PredicateFalse, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrEq'

// ⭐ **THE ONE BYTE-RANGE COMPARE LOOP.** Emit "every byte of `aBuf[0..len)` equals `bBuf[0..len)`" as a loop
// off `cur`: `cur` is terminated into a fresh header, a full match falls out to `allMatch`, and the first
// mismatching byte to `notEqual`. `len == 0` matches vacuously (the header guard is `i >= len`, true at once).
//
// ⚠ **SHARED BECAUSE TWO RUNTIME FUNCTIONS COMPARE BYTE RANGES**: `__str_eq` compares two whole records
// after a length check, and `Array.equals` compares two element buffers after the same length check
// (array-hashable — an array's equality IS a byte compare, `stdlib/Array.maxon:648-663`). They were one loop
// written twice, with nothing making them agree — a widened load or a changed bound in one would silently
// leave the other reading a byte at a time on exactly one of the paths. (A THIRD consumer,
// `__str_range_eq`, retired with the P1.8 Slice C searches at W49 wave 5; the sharing outlives it because
// the two that remain are still two.)
//
// ⚠ It stays a shared EMITTER and does NOT become a shared CALL: `==` on Strings is what the operator
// lowers to, and routing it through a call would buy this dedup at the price of a call per comparison. The
// duplication being removed is in the COMPILER's source, which is where it was.
//
// The loop index `i` is the header's phi — 0 from `cur`, `i+1` from the back edge — the on-the-fly SSA shape
// every runtime loop here builds directly, a runtime function having no parser. The phi's `argType` is set
// explicitly because `retypeBlockArgs` runs in `lowerMaxonToStd`, which these post-pipeline functions never
// pass through.
export function emitByteRangeCompareLoop(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, aBuf ValueId, bBuf ValueId, len ValueId, allMatch IrBlock, notEqual IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cmphdr")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cmpbody")

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: i, b: len, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: allMatch.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	// The compare is the LAST flag writer before the branch, so it fuses to a `jcc`.
	let aByte = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: body, base: aBuf, index: i, ids: ids)
	let bByte = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: body, base: bBuf, index: i, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iNext = emitBin(module, block: body, a: i, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let byteEq = emitCmp(module, block: body, a: aByte, b: bByte, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(byteEq, thenBlockId: header.id, elseBlockId: notEqual.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))
end 'emitByteRangeCompareLoop'

// ⭐ **WHAT THE SIGNED RENDERING DERIVES IN `setup` AND THE SHARED GRAPH THEN READS — and what the
// UNSIGNED one has instead, which is nothing at all (X11).** These two values are the ENTIRETY of the
// difference between the two renderings beyond the divides' own signedness, which is why one builder
// emits both: an unsigned rendering writes no `-`, its digit COUNT is already its length, and each
// `value % 10` is already the digit.
//
// It is a union rather than two `ValueId`s plus a flag because `unsigned` has no ids to carry, and a
// pair of ids that are meaningless in one arm is a sentinel wearing a struct's clothes.
union RenderedSign
	// `neg` — the sign bit as 0/1, which is also the digits' START OFFSET — and `sign`, `+1`/`-1`.
	signed(neg ValueId, sign ValueId)
	unsigned
end 'RenderedSign'

// `__int_to_string(value, dest) -> len` / `__uint_to_string(value, dest) -> len` — render `value`'s
// decimal text into `dest` at the signedness `signedness` names, returning the byte count written (never
// a NUL — the caller's buffer is zeroed and it copies exactly `len` bytes).
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE BUILDER, TWO ENTRY POINTS, AND THAT IS THE POINT (X11).** The two graphs share their entry
// zero case, their digit-COUNT loop and their digit-WRITE loop — the whole of the algorithm — and differ
// in exactly three places, all of them the sign: what `setup` derives (`RenderedSign`), whether the digit
// count is widened to a length, and whether each digit is multiplied back into its magnitude. Copying the
// 90 lines below to write the unsigned twin would put the shared 90 in two places where a fix to the
// count loop could reach one of them.
//
// ⭐ IT NEVER NEGATES, so `i64.min` needs no special case. `neg = value >>logical 63` is the sign bit as a
// 0/1. A leading `-` is written UNCONDITIONALLY at `dest[0]` and then OVERWRITTEN by the most-significant
// digit when the value is non-negative (that digit lands at `dest[start]` with `start = neg = 0`), so the
// sign costs no branch. Each digit is `value % 10` — which carries the value's sign — turned positive by
// MULTIPLYING by `sign = 1 - 2*neg` (`+1`/`-1`), again avoiding the `abs` that `i64.min` would overflow.
//
// ⚠ **THE SIGNED READING IS THIS GRAPH'S CONTRACT AND THE UNSIGNED ONE IS NOT A RELAXATION OF IT.** The
// divides below take their signedness from the parameter, and it must be the SAME reading the sign
// handling above takes: an unsigned `/10` under a `-` already written at `dest[0]` would emit the
// magnitude's digits while the sign still claimed a negative, and a signed `/10` on a value with bit 63
// set is X11's defect itself.
//
// Values dominating from `entry`/`setup` (`value`, `dest`, `neg`, `sign`) are read directly in the loop
// blocks those dominate; only the loop-variant `digits`/`v`/`pos` ride block-args. `len` is computed once
// in `writeSetup`, which dominates `done`.
function buildIntegerToString(module StdModule, signedness IntegerSignedness)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(integerToStringName(signedness), returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var zeroCase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"zeroCase")
	var setup = module.addBlock(func, label: b"setup")
	var countLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"countLoop")
	var writeSetup = module.addBlock(func, label: b"writeSetup")
	// The write loop is HEADER-GUARDED (test at the top), following `buildStrEq`/`buildStrCopy`'s shape so
	// the three string builders read the same way. It is a STYLE choice, not a correctness one. A fused
	// loop-test `cmp` whose boolean is a function's highest-numbered value once tripped the value-class
	// column — `setValueClass` sized it off the target OPERANDS, and a flags-only compare is not among them
	// — and this shape happened to dodge it by keeping the loop body's values minted last. That column now
	// GROWS to cover every DEFINED id (see `setValueClass` in StdToX64Conversion), so the dodge is
	// unnecessary and a bottom-tested loop would compile too; the shared shape stays for consistency.
	var writeHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"writeHeader")
	var writeBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"writeBody")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let value = 0 as ValueId
	let dest = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: `value == 0` prints a lone '0' (the count/write loops assume at least one non-zero step).
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isZero, lhs: value, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isZero, thenBlockId: zeroCase.id, elseBlockId: setup.id))

	let zeroDigitConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroCase, op: StdOp.const(zeroDigitConst, value: AsciiZeroDigit, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(zeroCase, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dest, offset: 0, valueId: zeroDigitConst, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let oneLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(zeroCase, op: StdOp.const(oneLen, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(zeroCase, op: StdOp.ret(oneLen))

	// setup: derive `neg` (0/1) and `sign` (+1/-1), and write the provisional '-' at dest[0] — or, for the
	// unsigned rendering, none of that.
	let renderedSign = emitRenderedSign(module, block: setup, ids: ids, value: value, dest: dest, signedness: signedness)
	let digitsZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(setup, op: StdOp.const(digitsZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(setup, op: StdOp.branch(countLoop.id))

	// countLoop(digits, v): count decimal digits — do { v = v/10; digits += 1 } while v != 0.
	let digits = ids.mint()
	let v = ids.mint()
	countLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(digits, argType: StdType.i64))
	countLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(v, argType: StdType.i64))
	setup.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(digitsZero, second: value)))

	let radixCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.const(radixCount, value: DecimalRadix, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let vNext = ids.mint()
	// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE READING THE SIGN HANDLING ABOVE TOOK** (X10 made every `StdOp.div` state its reading
	// rather than inherit one; X11 made the reading a parameter). For the SIGNED rendering the sign is
	// handled by `neg`/`sign` and each digit step is multiplied back, so the walk is signed by
	// construction; for the UNSIGNED one there is no sign to handle and bit 63 is a magnitude bit. The two
	// halves cannot be mixed — see this builder's header.
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.div(vNext, lhs: v, rhs: radixCount, signedness: signedness))
	let oneCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.const(oneCount, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digitsNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.binOp(digitsNext, lhs: digits, rhs: oneCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let countZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.const(countZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let countDone = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(countLoop, op: StdOp.cmp(countDone, lhs: vNext, rhs: countZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(countLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(countDone, thenBlockId: writeSetup.id, elseBlockId: countLoop.id))
	countLoop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(digitsNext, second: vNext)))

	// writeSetup: len = digits + neg; the last write position is len - 1. `digitsNext` is read straight —
	// `countLoop` defines it and dominates `writeSetup`, so the last iteration's count reaches here with no
	// exit phi (only the loop-carried `digits`/`v` recurrence needs a header phi). Then fall into the write
	// loop with the cursor at `lastPos` and the value to render.
	let len = emitSignWidenedLength(module, block: writeSetup, ids: ids, digits: digitsNext, sign: renderedSign)
	let oneWrite = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeSetup, op: StdOp.const(oneWrite, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let lastPos = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeSetup, op: StdOp.binOp(lastPos, lhs: len, rhs: oneWrite, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(writeSetup, op: StdOp.branch(writeHeader.id))

	// writeHeader(pos, wv): stop when `wv == 0` — every digit has been consumed. The top-tested guard is
	// what keeps `writeBody`'s materialized values (not this `cmp`) the last minted; see the block header.
	let pos = ids.mint()
	let wv = ids.mint()
	writeHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	writeHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(wv, argType: StdType.i64))
	writeSetup.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(writeHeader.id, argIds: twoArgs(lastPos, second: value)))

	let writeZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeHeader, op: StdOp.const(writeZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let writeDone = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeHeader, op: StdOp.cmp(writeDone, lhs: wv, rhs: writeZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(writeHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(writeDone, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: writeBody.id))

	// writeBody: dest[pos] = '0' + (wv % 10)*sign, then wv /= 10, pos -= 1, and back to the header. `pos`
	// and `wv` are read straight from `writeHeader`, which dominates this block.
	let radixWrite = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(radixWrite, value: DecimalRadix, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digit = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.mod(digit, lhs: wv, rhs: radixWrite, signedness: signedness))
	let digitAbs = emitDigitMagnitude(module, block: writeBody, ids: ids, digit: digit, sign: renderedSign)
	let asciiZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(asciiZero, value: AsciiZeroDigit, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digitChar = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(digitChar, lhs: digitAbs, rhs: asciiZero, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let writeAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(writeAddr, lhs: dest, rhs: pos, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(writeAddr, offset: 0, valueId: digitChar, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let wvRadix = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(wvRadix, value: DecimalRadix, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let wvNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.div(wvNext, lhs: wv, rhs: wvRadix, signedness: signedness))
	let oneWriteStep = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.const(oneWriteStep, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let posNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(writeBody, op: StdOp.binOp(posNext, lhs: pos, rhs: oneWriteStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(writeBody, op: StdOp.branch(writeHeader.id))
	writeBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(writeHeader.id, argIds: twoArgs(posNext, second: wvNext)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.ret(len))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildIntegerToString'

// The SIGNED rendering's `setup`: derive `neg` (0/1) and `sign` (+1/-1) and write the provisional `-` at
// `dest[0]`. The unsigned rendering emits NO OP AT ALL — not a `const 0` and a `const 1` for the shared
// graph to multiply by, which would cost a needless multiply per digit and an add per call for two values
// that are identities. See `RenderedSign`.
function emitRenderedSign(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, value ValueId, dest ValueId, signedness IntegerSignedness) returns RenderedSign
	// A `match` rather than an equality test, so a third reading added to `IntegerSignedness` fails to
	// compile here instead of silently taking the signed path and writing a `-` nothing asked for.
	match signedness 'reading'
		unsigned then return RenderedSign.unsigned
		signed then break 'reading'
	end 'reading'

	let signBitConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(signBitConst, value: SignBitPosition, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let neg = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(neg, lhs: value, rhs: signBitConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let minusConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(minusConst, value: AsciiMinusSign, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dest, offset: 0, valueId: minusConst, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let spanConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(spanConst, value: SignMultiplierSpan, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let twoNeg = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(twoNeg, lhs: spanConst, rhs: neg, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let signBaseConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(signBaseConst, value: SignMultiplierBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let sign = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(sign, lhs: signBaseConst, rhs: twoNeg, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))

	return RenderedSign.signed(neg, sign: sign)
end 'emitRenderedSign'

// `len = digits + neg` — the rendered width, which for an unsigned rendering IS the digit count, since
// `neg` is also the offset the most significant digit is written at.
function emitSignWidenedLength(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, digits ValueId, sign RenderedSign) returns ValueId
	return match sign 'rendering'
		unsigned gives digits
		signed(neg, _) gives emitI64BinOp(module, block: block, ids: ids, lhs: digits, rhs: neg, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add)
	end 'rendering'
end 'emitSignWidenedLength'

// `digit * sign` — one digit's magnitude. A SIGNED `value % 10` carries the value's sign, so a negative
// value's digits arrive negative and are multiplied back; an unsigned remainder is already the magnitude.
function emitDigitMagnitude(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, digit ValueId, sign RenderedSign) returns ValueId
	return match sign 'rendering'
		unsigned gives digit
		signed(_, multiplier) gives emitI64BinOp(module, block: block, ids: ids, lhs: digit, rhs: multiplier, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul)
	end 'rendering'
end 'emitDigitMagnitude'

// Mint a value and append the 64-bit `binOp` that defines it. The two arms above need that pair as an
// EXPRESSION — a `gives` arm is one — where the builders spell it out as two statements.
function emitI64BinOp(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode) returns ValueId
	let result = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(result, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: StdType.i64))
	return result
end 'emitI64BinOp'

// `__bool_to_string(value, dest) -> len` — render a bool as "true" (4 bytes) or "false" (5). The value is
// 0 or 1, so a single branch picks the rendering; each branch writes its bytes with fixed offsets and
// returns its length. The last value minted in each branch is the length const (returned, so materialized),
// never the entry `cmp`, so the value-class column is sized right (see `buildIntToString`'s header).
function buildBoolToString(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(BoolToStringName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var trueCase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"trueCase")
	var falseCase = module.addBlock(func, label: b"falseCase")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let value = 0 as ValueId
	let dest = 1 as ValueId

	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isFalse = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isFalse, lhs: value, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isFalse, thenBlockId: falseCase.id, elseBlockId: trueCase.id))

	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerT)
	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 1 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerR)
	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 2 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerU)
	storeConstByte(module, block: trueCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 3 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerE)
	let trueLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(trueCase, op: StdOp.const(trueLen, value: BoolTrueLength, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(trueCase, op: StdOp.ret(trueLen))

	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 0 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerF)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 1 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerA)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 2 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerL)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 3 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerS)
	storeConstByte(module, block: falseCase, ids: ids, dest: dest, offset: 4 as ByteOffset, charValue: AsciiLowerE)
	let falseLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(falseCase, op: StdOp.const(falseLen, value: BoolFalseLength, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(falseCase, op: StdOp.ret(falseLen))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildBoolToString'

// `__str_decref(record)` — a String's DROP. It is `__mm_decref` (dec the refcount; free at the last
// owner) plus the one thing the generic decref structurally cannot do: release the EXTERNAL buffer of a
// detached String BEFORE the record. The parser routes every owned-String drop here by TAG (`emitDecref`),
// so a struct box and a raw scratch buffer still drop through `__mm_decref`; only a String record, which
// alone carries the buffer-ownership encoding, drops through here.
//
// ⭐ **STATIC DISPATCH, NOT A DESTRUCTOR POINTER.** The record stores NO destructor — the parser knows the
// static type at every drop, so there is nothing to dispatch dynamically, and `funcAddr`/`callIndirect`
// stay deferred until a struct/array FIELD can hold a String (P1.4+), where a box's own `__mm_free` must
// reach a destructor it cannot name. Shipping the indirect-call vocabulary now would be vocabulary ahead
// of its forcing consumer — the mistake `__mm_incref` and `mintPhi` are the standing warnings against.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ITS FIRST QUESTION IS WHETHER THE RECORD IS COUNTABLE AT ALL, AND THAT ARM IS `__managed_decref`'s,
// SPELLED THE SAME WAY (W157 review).** A String and a Character LITERAL are `.rdata` records — blob and
// record both — and the PE writer maps that section READ-ONLY, so the refcount read-modify-write below is an
// access violation rather than a miscount, and the last-owner path would `__mm_free` an image address.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT GUARDS A DIVERGENCE, NOT A MEASURED CRASH — AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT.** No legal program
// reaches it: MEASURED by building this function with an `osExit(75)` in this arm's place and finding **zero
// hits across 1,636 spec cases** (string, ownership, arrays, managed-memory, character, file, slice, closure)
// **and ten hand-written literal-ownership shapes** — array element, struct field, field reassignment,
// parameter-into-field, union payload, tuple, `var` reassignment, array literal, module-level `let`, ternary.
// `return "literal"` COPIES into a fresh inline record (read out of the emitted IR: `__mm_alloc(len + 57)`,
// `capacity@16 = -3`), and `__str_retain` clones an immortal at every other door.
// ✅ The probe was proved LIVE by INVERTING its polarity — an ordinary interpolated String's drop then exits
// 75 — so the zero is an absence and not a mis-wired branch.
//
// ⇒ **WHAT IT BUYS IS THAT TWO DECREFS OVER ONE RECORD SHAPE STOP DISAGREEING.** Since W157 a String record
// IS a buffer record, so one record can be handed to `__managed_decref` (through `addressableBytes()`) or to
// this one, and `__managed_decref` skips an immortal. An asymmetry there is safe only by REACHABILITY, which
// is a premise about the corpus rather than an invariant. The arm answers `retVoid` and deliberately NOT an
// abort, for the same reason: its twin answers `retVoid`, and two drops of one record disagreeing about the
// ANSWER is the defect being closed, not a stricter check to add.
//
// The refcount read-modify-write is the shared `emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner` (load rc; `== 0` ⇒ last
// owner; else lower and keep) — the ONE home of the refcount protocol, so a String and a struct box
// cannot disagree about who the last owner is. `__str_decref` runs that shared check itself, rather
// than calling `__mm_decref` at runtime, because it must read `parent`/`buffer` out of the record on
// the last-owner path BEFORE that record is freed — the generic decref would reclaim the box first.
function buildStrDecref(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrDecrefName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var immortal = module.addBlock(func, label: b"immortal")
	var countable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"countable")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"baseJoin")
	var freeRec = module.addBlock(func, label: b"freeRec")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0 as ValueId

	// entry: is this record countable at all? An immortal `.rdata` record is left untouched — the header's
	// argument, and `ManagedMemoryRuntime.__managed_decref`'s arm spelled off the same one test.
	let isImmortal = emitRecordIsImmortal(module, block: entry, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isImmortal, thenBlockId: immortal.id, elseBlockId: countable.id))
	module.setTerminator(immortal, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// countable + lower: the shared refcount check. `rc == 0` (the last owner) branches to `release`, where a
	// String does the one thing the generic decref structurally cannot — release a detached buffer first.
	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, func: func, entry: countable, lower: lower, record: record, lastOwner: release, ids: ids)

	// release + baseJoin: the last owner asks the shared pair which separate allocation this record owes a
	// release to and pays it, BEFORE the record itself dies — the whole reason this cannot simply call
	// `__mm_decref`, which would reclaim the box while its `capacity`/`buffer` are still the answer. A
	// String that detached is an OWNER (`capacity@16 >= 0`) and owes its own `buffer@0`; one whose bytes are
	// inline (or in an immortal `.rdata` blob) has a negative capacity and leaves through the `parent@32`
	// arm holding `NoOwedAllocation`, which the null guard inside the release step turns into an ordinary
	// answer rather than a case. It is the SAME pair `__managed_decref` uses, so a String and an Array cannot
	// disagree about what a record owns.
	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: release, join: baseJoin, record: record, ids: ids)
	var afterRelease = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: baseJoin, base: base, callee: MmDecrefName, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterRelease, op: StdOp.branch(freeRec.id))

	// freeRec: release the record itself, its external bytes (if it had any) already released.
	let recFreed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(freeRec, op: StdOp.call(recFreed, callee: MmFreeName, args: argsOf(record)))
	module.setTerminator(freeRec, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrDecref'

// ⭐ **THE ONE ENCODER of shv2's fused OWNED inline String record** — record + inline bytes + a trailing NUL in
// ONE `__mm_alloc`, `buffer@0` pointing at the inline region, `capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`, `parent@32`
// left 0 by the slab zeroing, and `singleByteGraphemes@48 = singleByteGraphemes`. Emits the ops into `block` and returns the record.
// Shared so the record layout has exactly ONE construction site: `__str_clone` (passes the SOURCE's `singleByteGraphemes`)
// and `__gt_subp_str_from_bytes` (passes 0 — child output is arbitrary bytes, `not-ascii` is the safe class)
// were byte-for-byte copies of this sequence, and a field the record later gains must be initialized once, here,
// not silently in one path and not the other.
export function emitBuildOwnedInlineString(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, srcBuf ValueId, srcLen ValueId, singleByteGraphemes ValueId) returns ValueId
	let newRec = emitAllocOwnedInlineString(module, block: block, ids: ids, byteLen: srcLen, singleByteGraphemes: singleByteGraphemes)
	let copied = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(copied, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(emitInlineStringBuffer(module, block: block, ids: ids, record: newRec), second: srcBuf, third: srcLen)))
	return newRec
end 'emitBuildOwnedInlineString'

// ⭐ **THE RECORD HALF of `emitBuildOwnedInlineString`, split out for the producers that do NOT have one
// contiguous source to copy from.** `__str_replace` was the original — it assembled its result from
// alternating slices of the receiver and of the replacement — and it retired onto `stdlib/String.maxon` in
// W49 wave 2; `GraphemeRuntime.emitCharRecordBuffer` is the reader that remains, and it writes a codepoint's
// UTF-8 bytes one at a time. Both need the box PUBLISHED and the bytes left to them. Everything about the
// record is settled here — its size (`byteLen` + record + NUL), its `buffer@0`, and the whole buffer-ownership
// encoding (`capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`, `parent@32` left 0 by the slab's zeroing contract) — so a
// caller that fills the bytes itself cannot publish a record that differs from a cloned one by a field.
export function emitAllocOwnedInlineString(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, byteLen ValueId, singleByteGraphemes ValueId) returns ValueId
	// One `__mm_alloc` for the record + the bytes + a trailing NUL (the slab zeroes it), sized exactly as an
	// interpolation of a single part is (`StringInterpOverheadBytes` = record + NUL).
	let overhead = emitConst(module, block: block, value: StringInterpOverheadBytes, ids: ids)
	let allocSize = emitBin(module, block: block, a: byteLen, b: overhead, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	// A fused String record owns nothing beyond the one allocation it IS, so nothing cascades — the same
	// `TrivialDestructor` the parser passes at `emitBoxAlloc`, read from its one declaration rather than
	// spelled as a bare 0 (`__strix_make` is the third reader of it).
	let zeroDtor = emitConst(module, block: block, value: TrivialDestructor, ids: ids)
	let newRec = ids.mint()
	emitMmAllocCall(module, block: block, result: newRec, size: allocSize, destructor: zeroDtor, tag: mmTagIndex(MmTag.StringRecord), ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: newRec, offset: StringBufferOffset, value: emitInlineStringBuffer(module, block: block, ids: ids, record: newRec))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: newRec, offset: StringLengthOffset, value: byteLen)
	let capacity = emitConst(module, block: block, value: InlineBufferCapacity as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: newRec, offset: StringCapacityOffset, value: capacity)
	let elementSize = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ByteStringElementSize, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: newRec, offset: StringElementSizeOffset, value: elementSize)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: newRec, offset: StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset, value: singleByteGraphemes)

	return newRec
end 'emitAllocOwnedInlineString'

// Where a fused record's inline bytes begin: right after the record. ONE definition, asked for by everything
// that writes those bytes — so a caller re-deriving it (two ops, a const and an add, in a compiler-internal
// graph) is cheaper than a second place that could be told a different record size.
export function emitInlineStringBuffer(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, record ValueId) returns ValueId
	let recordBytes = emitConst(module, block: block, value: StringRecordBytes as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: record, b: recordBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitInlineStringBuffer'

// ⭐ **`__str_clone(src) -> newStr` — A STRING'S DEEP CLONE (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b).** A fresh OWNED heap String,
// an independent copy of `src` that `__str_decref` reclaims without touching `src`. It is the SAME single-
// `__mm_alloc` fused-record path an interpolation builds (`Parser.emitStringInterp` / `promoteToOwnedString`,
// with the source as its one part), packaged as a runtime function so the managed array/struct clone walk can
// take its `funcAddr` and `callIndirect` it per element/field.
//
// ⭐ **THE RESULT IS INLINE, so it is INDEPENDENTLY DROPPABLE with no double-free of the source's bytes.** One
// `__mm_alloc` holds the 56-byte record with the bytes right behind it (`buffer@0 = newRec + StringRecordBytes`),
// `capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity` and `parent@32 = NoOwedAllocation` (the slab's zeroing supplies the 0) —
// the same encoding `emitStringInterp` publishes — so `__str_decref` reclaims record and bytes together and owes
// nothing to anyone. A LITERAL source (immortal `.rdata`, `capacity == ImmortalRecordCapacity`) clones through this
// identical path: its bytes are COPIED into the new inline box, so the clone frees its own copy and never the
// immortal blob. `singleByteGraphemes@48` is carried over from the source, an accurate copy at no extra cost.
function buildStrClone(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrCloneName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let src = 0 as ValueId

	// A clone PRESERVES the source's `singleByteGraphemes` classification (the bytes are copied verbatim), unlike a
	// raw-bytes construction which cannot know it.
	let srcLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let srcBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let srcSingleByte = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset, ids: ids)
	let newRec = emitBuildOwnedInlineString(module, block: entry, ids: ids, srcBuf: srcBuf, srcLen: srcLen, singleByteGraphemes: srcSingleByte)

	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(newRec))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrClone'

// `__str_retain(record) -> ownedRecord` — the two-armed graph the constant's header argues for.
//
// ⚠ **THE TEST IS `== ImmortalRecordCapacity` AND MUST NOT BE `emitBufferNotOwned`'s `capacity < 0`.** That
// one asks *"is this buffer mine to write in place?"*, which is FALSE for an ordinary owned INLINE String
// too (`InlineBufferCapacity`) — and an inline String is heap, refcounted and exactly what the incref arm
// exists for. Asking the wider question would clone every owned String reaching a merge, which is a lost
// write, not merely a cost. This is the one provenance test that wants a specific value rather than a sign.
function buildStrRetain(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrRetainName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var copyImmortal = module.addBlock(func, label: b"copy")
	var coOwn = module.addBlock(func, label: b"coown")
	var join = module.addBlock(func, label: b"owned")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0 as ValueId

	let isImmortal = emitRecordIsImmortal(module, block: entry, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isImmortal, thenBlockId: copyImmortal.id, elseBlockId: coOwn.id))

	// The two arms answer with DIFFERENT records — the clone's fresh one, the incref's own — so the answer
	// is a block arg rather than a value either arm could write into the other's name.
	let owned = ids.mint()
	join.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(owned, argType: StdType.i64))

	let copied = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyImmortal, op: StdOp.call(copied, callee: StrCloneName, args: argsOf(record)))
	module.setTerminator(copyImmortal, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	copyImmortal.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(copied)))

	let increfed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(coOwn, op: StdOp.call(increfed, callee: MmIncrefName, args: argsOf(record)))
	module.setTerminator(coOwn, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	coOwn.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: argsOf(record)))

	module.setTerminator(join, op: StdOp.ret(owned))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrRetain'

// ⚠ **THE `--- P1.8 Slice C ---` SECTION WAS HERE AND IS EMPTY** (W49 wave 5). It held `__str_range_eq`
// and `__str_find` to the end: the raw byte-range compare and the positioned search that `startsWith`,
// `endsWith`, `contains`, `split` and the two `StringIndex` searches were all built on. Every one of those
// members is `stdlib/String.maxon`'s now, and the corpus reaches the same two answers through its own
// `bytesEqual`/`findIn` — which these graphs were transcribed from. See the P1.8 Slice C grave at the head
// of this file for why they could not be deleted one wave sooner.

// ⭐ **`__str_from_bytes(arr) -> newStr` — the fused-wrapper literal whose declaration carries NO flag
// field to pass (`Character{managed: X}`); see the constant's header.** The argument's buffer holds exactly
// the UTF-8 bytes the record is to have, so the whole of the work is the classification below and the same
// fused-record build every other owned String is born through.
//
// ⚠ **WHY IT COPIES IS STATED ONCE, AT `StrOfBufferName`, AND THE VERSION THAT USED TO BE HERE IS
// RETRACTED.** This paragraph read *"shv2 has no shared-ownership relationship between an `Array` record
// and a `String` record"*; `buildStrBytesView` established exactly that relationship at the P1.7 COW slice,
// so the divergence is a CHOICE with a failure mode behind it, not a thing shv2 cannot do. Two headers
// arguing one point is how one of them came to be false for a whole slice without anything noticing.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE PER-BYTE COST OF THE MEMBER THIS GRAPH USED TO SERVE MOVED WITH THE BODY — RECORDED, NOT
// CURED** (W55, and the same ruling the `findIn`/`sliceBytes` note at the head of this file carries). The
// corpus's `scanSingleByteGraphemes` (`stdlib/String.maxon:1062-1071`) asks `managed.byteAt(i)` — a
// throwing, bounds-checked accessor lowering to a `__managed_byte_at` CALL — once per byte, where the loop
// below walks the same bytes with an inline indexed load. shv2 has no inliner, so that is a real call per
// byte for every `String.from(bytes)` and every `String.init(managed)` in a program. ⛔ **THE LADDER
// CANNOT SEE IT AND A Δ0 FROM IT IS NOT EVIDENCE**: the generated scale corpus emits neither call, so every
// column reads flat — the CORPUS blind spot, which the CPU column does not cure.
function buildStrFromBytes(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrFromBytesName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var scan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scan")
	var scanBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanBody")
	var notHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notHigh")
	var stillSingleByte = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stillSingleByte")
	var sawMultiByteGrapheme = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sawMultiByteGrapheme")
	var build = module.addBlock(func, label: b"build")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	let len = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let buf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))

	// The `singleByteGraphemes@48` classification, computed rather than guessed: it is a claim about these
	// bytes, and the one place a String's is otherwise produced (`bytesAreSingleByteGraphemes`) makes the
	// identical claim at lower time for a literal. It is carried as an i64 0/1, the width the field is stored
	// at, which is why each disqualifying case is a BRANCH rather than an `and` of the `i1` a compare yields:
	// the two are different Std types and the flag must be the record's.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO TESTS PER BYTE, NOT ONE, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE THIS FLAG IS NAMED FOR.** A high byte begins
	// a multi-byte codepoint; a CR is ASCII yet is not a grapheme on its own, because GB3 joins it to a
	// following LF. Both are disqualifying and they are asked in a LADDER for the reason above — the `and` of
	// two `i1`s would not be the i64 the field holds.
	let i = ids.mint()
	let singleByte = ids.mint()
	scan.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	scan.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(singleByte, argType: StdType.i64))
	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: one)))

	let scanned = emitCmp(module, block: scan, a: i, b: len, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scan, op: StdOp.condBranch(scanned, thenBlockId: build.id, elseBlockId: scanBody.id))

	let raw = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: scanBody, base: buf, index: i, ids: ids)
	let highBit = emitConst(module, block: scanBody, value: AsciiHighBit, ids: ids)
	let isLow = emitCmp(module, block: scanBody, a: raw, b: highBit, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let iNext = emitBin(module, block: scanBody, a: i, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isLow, thenBlockId: notHigh.id, elseBlockId: sawMultiByteGrapheme.id))

	let crByte = emitConst(module, block: notHigh, value: CarriageReturnByte as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	let isNotCr = emitCmp(module, block: notHigh, a: raw, b: crByte, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notHigh, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNotCr, thenBlockId: stillSingleByte.id, elseBlockId: sawMultiByteGrapheme.id))

	module.setTerminator(stillSingleByte, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))
	stillSingleByte.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: twoArgs(iNext, second: singleByte)))

	module.setTerminator(sawMultiByteGrapheme, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))
	sawMultiByteGrapheme.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: twoArgs(iNext, second: zero)))

	module.setTerminator(build, op: StdOp.ret(emitBuildOwnedInlineString(module, block: build, ids: ids, srcBuf: buf, srcLen: len, singleByteGraphemes: singleByte)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrFromBytes'

// ⭐ **`__str_of_buffer(srcRecord, singleByteGraphemes) -> String`** — see the constant's header for what it
// is for and why it copies. The whole body is `buildStrFromBytes`'s tail with the scan removed: the flag
// arrives as a parameter, so there is no ladder and no loop, just the read of the source's two buffer slots
// and the one shared record build.
//
// ⚠ **THE SOURCE IS READ THROUGH THE *ARRAY* SLOTS, AND THAT IS THE HONEST SPELLING RATHER THAN A COINCIDENCE
// EXPLOITED.** Its argument is a `__ManagedMemory`, which in shv2 IS the array-shaped buffer record — what
// `arr.managed` yields and what `managed.slice(…)` returns (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitViewRecordHead` builds it
// through `__managed_create`). The two records agree at `buffer@0`/`length@8`, and `BufferOwnership`'s header
// says in so many words that *"the two sets coincide today and nothing here relies on that"* — so reading a
// buffer through the buffer record's own constants is what keeps that true.
function buildStrOfBuffer(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrOfBufferName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let src = 0 as ValueId
	let singleByteGraphemes = 1 as ValueId

	let len = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let buf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(emitBuildOwnedInlineString(module, block: entry, ids: ids, srcBuf: buf, srcLen: len, singleByteGraphemes: singleByteGraphemes)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrOfBuffer'

// ⭐ **`__mm_to_cstring(bufRecord) -> ptr`** — see the constant's header for the contract and for why the
// answer needs no owner. Four blocks: decide whether `buffer[length]` may be READ at all, read it when it
// may, hand the buffer straight back when it is already 0, and otherwise grow the record by one byte and
// terminate it IN PLACE.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE PROBE READS ONE BYTE PAST THE CONTENT AND MAY ONLY DO SO FOR A RECORD THAT CAN VOUCH FOR IT —
// `BufferOwnership.emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot` IS THAT DECISION, AND IT USED TO BE MISSING.** The
// four shapes it admits, the two it refuses (a VIEW, and an EXACTLY-FULL owned buffer — which is what a
// first `String.append` leaves behind, not a corner case), and the bootstrap twin that carries the same
// rule are all written down there rather than repeated here.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SECOND PATH MUTATES THE RECEIVER, WHICH IS THE PART TO GET RIGHT.** It is a COW: the bytes,
// the length and every answer any reader can observe are unchanged, and what moves is `buffer@0` /
// `capacity@16` / `parent@32` — a view becomes an owner of its own copy, an rdata buffer becomes a heap
// one, and an exactly-full buffer gains slack. Every record that can arrive here has a WRITABLE record, so
// the republish cannot fault: the router's `unvouched` set is a view, an exactly-full owner and any future
// untaught provenance, all of them ordinary `__mm_alloc` boxes; and of the shapes that arrive by FAILING
// the probe, `RdataBufferCapacity`'s record is a heap box too, while `InlineBufferCapacity`'s and
// `ImmortalRecordCapacity`'s cannot fail it at all (each is created with its terminator slot already zero
// — `emitAllocOwnedInlineString`'s `StringInterpOverheadBytes`, and `stringBlobBytes`'s NUL). That last one
// carries the whole weight for the one record in this language that lives in READ-ONLY `.rdata`, so it is
// stated rather than assumed: a String LITERAL never reaches the write below because its blob is
// NUL-terminated by construction.
function buildMmToCString(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(MmToCStringName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var probe = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nulprobe")
	var alreadyTerminated = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nulok")
	var terminateInPlace = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nulwrite")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let src = 0 as ValueId

	let len = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let buf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	emitRouteOnVouchedTerminatorSlot(module, func: func, cur: entry, record: src, length: len, vouched: probe, unvouched: terminateInPlace, ids: ids)

	// The load lives in `probe` and not in `entry` because the branch above IS its bounds check: emitted
	// beside the two field reads it would run for every record, which is the out-of-bounds read the router
	// exists to remove. `entry` dominates this block, so `buf` and `len` need no block argument.
	let terminator = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: probe, base: buf, index: len, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: probe, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isTerminated = emitCmp(module, block: probe, a: terminator, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(probe, op: StdOp.condBranch(isTerminated, thenBlockId: alreadyTerminated.id, elseBlockId: terminateInPlace.id))

	module.setTerminator(alreadyTerminated, op: StdOp.ret(buf))

	// Ask for `len + 1` ELEMENTS, which is `len + 1` BYTES for every record that can reach this door: a
	// String record's `element_size@24` is 1 and so is a `ByteArray`'s, which are the only two shapes
	// `toCString()` accepts (`Parser.requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod` guards the spelling; the `cstring` type
	// itself is a byte pointer).
	let one = emitConst(module, block: terminateInPlace, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let withNul = emitBin(module, block: terminateInPlace, a: len, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitManagedReserve(module, block: terminateInPlace, record: src, elementCount: withNul, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ **RE-READ `buffer@0`: THE RESERVE MAY HAVE MOVED IT**, and `buf` above is then the address it just
	// freed. The terminator is then WRITTEN rather than left to the fresh allocation's zeroing contract, so
	// that this block is correct on its own terms and not on the early-out of a callee that could return
	// without reallocating anything.
	let owned = emitLoadField(module, block: terminateInPlace, record: src, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let nulAddr = emitBin(module, block: terminateInPlace, a: owned, b: len, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let nul = emitConst(module, block: terminateInPlace, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(terminateInPlace, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(nulAddr, offset: 0, valueId: nul, valueType: StdType.u8))
	module.setTerminator(terminateInPlace, op: StdOp.ret(owned))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildMmToCString'

// ⭐ **`__mm_from_cstring(cstr) -> arrRecord`** — see the constant's header for the contract and for why it
// must copy. One block of its own: the strlen walk and the copy add their own, and `emitCStringToManaged`
// hands back the block execution continues in.
function buildMmFromCString(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(MmFromCStringName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let cstr = 0 as ValueId

	let record = ids.mint()
	let built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: entry, cstr: cstr, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildMmFromCString'

// ⭐⭐ **`__str_bytes_view(str) -> arrRecord` — `addressableBytes()`, AND SINCE W157 IT IS THE RECEIVER'S
// OWN RECORD, CO-OWNED.** The corpus's body is `return managed` (`stdlib/String.maxon:181-183`) — hand out
// this String's own buffer, do not copy it — and that is now literally what this does: one `__mm_incref`
// and the same pointer back. Nothing is allocated, nothing is copied, and the cost does not depend on the
// length.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT USED TO MINT A 48-BYTE VIEW, AND ONE SLOT IS WHY.** A String record and an Array record agreed at
// five of their six slots and DISAGREED at `@40`, where a String kept `singleByteGraphemes` and an Array
// keeps `element_destroy`. Handing the String pointer to the array dispatch therefore made
// `urlBytes.slice(0, colonPos)` build a view whose `element_destroy@40` was the source's ASCII FLAG, and
// `__managed_decref` would `callIndirect` address 1 on every element of an all-ASCII slice — every corpus
// call, since a URL is all-ASCII. **W157 removed the disagreement rather than the consequence**: a String
// record carries a real `element_destroy@40` (always `TrivialElementDestroy`, which is the truth about a
// buffer of `Byte`s) and its flag moved to `@48`, so the fresh record that used to STATE that fact is a
// record that no longer needs stating. It cost one 48-byte allocation per `addressableBytes()` — i.e. per
// `sb.append(...)`, per `print(...)`, per `File.exists(...)` — and it is gone.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE IMMORTAL ARM IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION, IT IS THE ONE CASE THAT WOULD FAULT.** A String LITERAL's
// record lives in `.rdata`, which the PE writer maps READ-ONLY (`SectionRdataFlags`), so `__mm_incref`'s
// refcount read-modify-write on it is an access violation rather than a miscount. That arm returns the
// record UNCOUNTED, which is sound for exactly the reason the memory is read-only: it can never be freed,
// so nothing needs to hold it. Its dual is in `__managed_decref`, which skips the whole drop on the same
// `ImmortalRecordCapacity` test — the two are one decision and must move together.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A WRITE THROUGH THIS BUFFER REACHES THE STRING NOW, WHERE THROUGH THE OLD VIEW IT WAS LOST.** The
// view's `capacity@16 = ViewBufferCapacity` sent every in-place mutator through `__managed_cow_detach`,
// which gave the CALLER a private copy that died at statement end — so the write silently vanished (the
// defect W49 wave 2 measured, when every case conversion in the suite returned its receiver verbatim). The
// receiver's own record detaches onto a buffer the STRING keeps, which is the same answer `String.setByte`
// already gets by taking the record. ⚠ An IMMORTAL receiver is the exception and it is a FAULT, not a lost
// write: `__managed_cow_detach` republishes `buffer@0`/`capacity@16`/`parent@32` into the record, and that
// record is read-only. The parser promotes a `var` String at its declaration and a `let` binding refuses
// the mutation, which is what keeps a literal off every writing path — the same guard that already stood
// between a literal and `String.setByte`.
//
// ⚠ **LIFETIME: the caller holds a REFERENCE COUNT on the record, which is one guarantee STRONGER than the
// view gave.** The view counted the allocation the bytes live in and left the record to fend for itself;
// counting the record covers both, because a record that cannot be freed cannot release its own external
// buffer either (`__str_decref` reaches that release only as the last owner). That is one more guarantee
// than the reference gives, where `return managed` relies on the caller keeping the String alive.
function buildStrBytesView(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(StrBytesViewName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var coOwn = module.addBlock(func, label: b"bvcoown")
	var ret = module.addBlock(func, label: b"bvret")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0 as ValueId

	let isImmortal = emitRecordIsImmortal(module, block: entry, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isImmortal, thenBlockId: ret.id, elseBlockId: coOwn.id))

	let increfed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(coOwn, op: StdOp.call(increfed, callee: MmIncrefName, args: argsOf(record)))
	module.setTerminator(coOwn, op: StdOp.branch(ret.id))

	module.setTerminator(ret, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrBytesView'

// ⚠⚠ **`buildStrByteRead` WAS HERE AND WENT WITH BOTH ITS CALLEES (W49 wave 7).** It was ONE bounds-checked
// byte read serving `__str_byte_at` and `__str_byte_at_or_panic` — the same graph wearing two failure exits,
// with the exit DERIVED from the name via `isThrowingStringRuntimeCallee` rather than passed beside it. That
// derivation is worth remembering even though the function is gone: it replaced a `throwing bool` parameter
// after the review MEASURED the divergent shape (stamp removed, predicate unchanged) leaving **all 13
// `stdlib-loading` cases GREEN**, because no committed input reached an out-of-range index. **A `throws`
// stamp that disagrees with the predicate deciding `try`-ness is invisible to a suite** — build the fact
// once and derive the rest.
//
// Both members are `stdlib/String.maxon:284` and `:299` now, and their bodies forward to
// `__ManagedMemory.byteAt`, whose own `__managed_byte_at` carries the identical bound and the identical
// `managedMemoryThrows()` clause. So the read did not lose its check; it moved to the one buffer entry point
// that always had it.
//
// ⚠ **`buildStrSplit` WAS HERE TOO, WITH THE FOUR EMITTERS IT WAS THE LAST CALLER OF** (W49 wave 5):
// `emitPushSliceSegment`, `emitSliceSegment`, `emitPushSegment` and `emitStrFindCall`. It filled a
// caller-created `Array with String` whose element size and `element_destroy@40` stamp the PARSER decided
// through `emitArrayCreateOp`; the corpus decides both through the ordinary generic-instance path and
// stamps them identically (measured — `Parser.stringSurfaceMemberNames` carries the emitted-code diff).
//
// ⚠ **TWO OF THE FOUR WERE `export`ED FOR CONSUMERS THAT HAD ALREADY GONE**, which is why they are named
// here rather than passed over: `emitSliceSegment`'s header claimed a THIRD consumer in `__strix_slice` and
// `emitStrFindCall`'s claimed one in `__strix_find`, both deleted with `StringIndexRuntime.maxon` at wave 4.
// An `export` outlives its last cross-file caller silently — nothing in this language reports one — so a
// retirement has to go looking for them by name.

// Store one constant byte at `dest[offset]`. The shape a fixed-text renderer (`__bool_to_string`) writes
// each of its bytes with — a `const` typed i64, stored as a `u8` (the store keeps the low byte).
function storeConstByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, dest ValueId, offset ByteOffset, charValue ParsedInt)
	let ch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(ch, value: charValue, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dest, offset: offset, valueId: ch, valueType: StdType.u8))
end 'storeConstByte'

// `twoArgs` / `threeArgs` — the pair and triple call/branch-edge builders — live in `MmRuntime` next
// to `argsOf`, shared by every runtime builder (see MmRuntime), along with `oneI64Param`/`twoI64Params`/
// `threeI64Params` and the single-op emit helpers every builder here reaches for.
